window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("titus", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"Titus","chapters":3,"bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Grace That Trains a People for Good Works","paragraphs":["Picture a rugged island in the Mediterranean Sea: Crete, with its mountains, harbours and many small cities. Cretans had been in Jerusalem on the day of Pentecost, hearing the apostles speak “the mighty works of God” (Acts 2:11), and churches had since sprung up across the island. But they were young, unorganised and vulnerable. False teachers were upsetting whole households for money. Even the island's own poet said its people had a reputation as liars and lazy gluttons. Into this setting Paul sent Titus, and then wrote him this short, powerful letter.","Titus was one of Paul's most trusted co-workers. He was a Greek, uncircumcised, whom Paul took to Jerusalem as living proof that Gentiles are saved by grace without the law: “But not even Titus, who was with me, being a Greek, was compelled to be circumcised” (Galatians 2:3). Later Paul sent him to sort out the troubled Corinthian church and to organise its giving. Paul calls him “my partner and fellow worker for you” (2 Corinthians 8:23). He was the man for hard assignments.","The letter was probably written between 1 Timothy and 2 Timothy, after Paul's release from his first Roman imprisonment, while he was travelling again. It shares many themes with 1 Timothy: appointing godly elders, silencing false teachers and teaching sound doctrine. But Titus is shorter and sharper. Its great theme is that truth produces godliness, and that grace produces good works. Paul uses the phrase ‘good works’ again and again, because a church in a place known for lies and laziness must show the beauty of the gospel by the way it lives.","At the heart of the letter are two of the richest summaries of the gospel in the New Testament. “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men” (Titus 2:11), training us to say no to ungodliness and to live godly lives while we wait for “the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13). And: “not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit” (Titus 3:5). Titus shows that grace not only saves us but also trains us."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"2 Timothy","intro":"The Holy Spirit placed Titus directly after 2 Timothy. Though Titus was most likely written earlier, it follows naturally: the dying apostle's charge to Timothy is matched by his instructions to another trusted son for building a healthy church from the ground up.","points":[{"title":"From Dalmatia Back to Crete","body":"Near the end of 2 Timothy, Paul notes that “Titus to Dalmatia” (2 Timothy 4:10) had gone on mission to the coast of the Adriatic. The letter to Titus takes us back to an earlier assignment on Crete: “I left you in Crete for this reason, that you would set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city, as I directed you” (Titus 1:5). The same faithful worker who stayed at his post on Crete was still serving at the end of Paul's life."},{"title":"From ‘They Will Not Listen to Sound Doctrine’ to ‘Say What Fits Sound Doctrine’","body":"2 Timothy warned, “For the time will come when they will not listen to the sound doctrine” (2 Timothy 4:3). Titus gives the positive programme: “But say the things which fit sound doctrine” (Titus 2:1). ‘Sound’ (ὑγιαίνω) means healthy. Titus is to teach healthy doctrine and to show what a healthy church looks like — old and young, men and women, slaves and free, all living lives that fit the truth."},{"title":"From ‘Equipped for Every Good Work’ to ‘Zealous for Good Works’","body":"2 Timothy said that God-breathed Scripture makes the man of God “thoroughly equipped for every good work” (2 Timothy 3:17). Titus shows what that equipment is for. Christ died to make for Himself “a people for his own possession, zealous for good works” (Titus 2:14). Believers must be “ready for every good work” (Titus 3:1) and “careful to maintain good works” (Titus 3:8). No New Testament letter mentions good works more often for its size."},{"title":"From ‘Loved His Appearing’ to ‘The Grace of God Has Appeared’","body":"2 Timothy promised a crown “to all those who have loved his appearing” (2 Timothy 4:8). Titus explains both appearings. The first: “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men” (Titus 2:11). The second: we are “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13). Christian life is lived between the two, trained by the first and longing for the second."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"Grace That Saves, Grace That Trains","anchorRef":"Titus 2:11–14; 3:4–8","paragraphs":["The letter's core is that the grace of God both saves and trains. “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men, instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (2:11–12). The word for ‘instructing’ (παιδεύω) means to train a child. Grace is not only a pardon; it is a teacher. It tells us what to say no to and what to say yes to.","Salvation itself is entirely God's work: “not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit” (3:5). We contribute nothing to it. God's “kindness” and His “love toward mankind” appeared in Christ (3:4); He poured out His Spirit richly (3:6); He justified us by grace and made us heirs of eternal life (3:7).","But the salvation that is not by works is always for good works. Christ “gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify for himself a people for his own possession, zealous for good works” (2:14). Immediately after the great summary of grace in chapter 3, Paul writes: “so that those who have believed God may be careful to maintain good works” (3:8). Good works are not the root of salvation, but they are its fruit.","That is why truth and life cannot be separated in this letter. It opens with “the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (1:1). The false teachers “profess that they know God, but by their deeds they deny him” (1:16). In contrast, the church must live in a way that will “adorn the doctrine of God, our Savior, in all things” (2:10). The beauty of the gospel is to be seen in the lives of ordinary believers."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 3 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"Healthy Doctrine Produces Healthy Lives","body":"Paul keeps using the word ‘sound’, which means healthy. Sound doctrine is not dry theory; it is what makes people spiritually healthy. Elders must hold to “the faithful word which is according to the teaching, that he may be able to exhort in the sound doctrine” (1:9). When the truth is taught and believed, it shapes character: sober-minded, self-controlled, loving, reverent and kind."},{"title":"Grace Is Your Teacher","body":"The grace that saves you also trains you. It teaches you to deny ungodliness and worldly desires and to live sensibly, righteously and godly now, while you wait for Christ's appearing (2:11–13). You are not left to change yourself by willpower. God's grace, given through Christ and applied by His Spirit, is patiently training you."},{"title":"Saved by Mercy, Ready for Good Works","body":"You were saved “not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy” (3:5). That frees you from trying to earn God's love. And it frees you to do good, not to be saved, but because you are saved. Believers are to be “ready for every good work” (3:1) — practical, useful, generous, and a blessing to the people around them."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Does Your Life Adorn the Gospel?","body":"Paul wants believers' lives to ‘adorn’ the teaching about God, as jewels adorn a crown. Think about your daily conduct at home, at work and in your community. Would those who watch you find the gospel more beautiful, or less? Ask God to show you one area where your life does not yet fit what you believe."},{"title":"Where Has Grace Been Training You?","body":"Grace is a teacher. Think back over the last year. Where has God been saying ‘no’ to something in your life, and ‘yes’ to something else? Thank Him for His patient training, and ask Him to make you teachable as you study this letter."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, I come to Your letter to Titus, written to strengthen young churches on an island known for lies and laziness. Thank You that Your grace has appeared, bringing salvation to all people. Thank You that You saved me not because of works I had done, but because of Your mercy, through the washing of new birth and the renewing of the Holy Spirit, whom You poured out on me richly through Jesus Christ my Saviour. Let Your grace train me to say no to ungodliness and worldly desires, and to live sensibly, righteously and godly in this present age. Give me a love for sound, healthy teaching. Make me sober-minded, self-controlled, a lover of good and ready for every good work. Let my life adorn the teaching about You in all things. Raise up godly leaders in Your church, and protect Your people from those who profess to know You but deny You by their deeds. And keep my eyes fixed on the blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Saviour, Jesus Christ. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Titus","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Truth That Leads to Godliness","themeTitle":"“God, Who Can’t Lie”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Paul, a servant of God, and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of God’s chosen ones, and the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness,","understanding":"Παῦλος δοῦλος θεοῦ, “Paul, a servant of God” — δοῦλος from the servant family: a slave, wholly owned. Only here does Paul call himself ‘a slave of God’; elsewhere it is ‘of Christ Jesus’. ἀπόστολος δὲ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “and an apostle of Jesus Christ” — a sent messenger. His ministry serves two things: κατὰ πίστιν ἐκλεκτῶν θεοῦ, “according to the faith of God’s chosen ones,” and ἐπίγνωσιν ἀληθείας τῆς κατ᾽ εὐσέβειαν, “and the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness.” Two families meet: ἐπίγνωσις from the know family (full, personal knowledge) and εὐσέβεια from the godliness family. True knowledge of God always leads to a godly life. That phrase is the key to the whole letter.","words":[{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen ones"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"}]},{"v":2,"text":"in hope of eternal life, which God, who can’t lie, promised before time began;","understanding":"ἐπ᾽ ἐλπίδι ζωῆς αἰωνίου, “in hope of eternal life” — ζωή from the life family: the life of God Himself, not merely biological life (βίος). ἣν ἐπηγγείλατο ὁ ἀψευδὴς θεός, “which God, who can’t lie, promised” — ἀψευδής, literally ‘un-lying’. Hold this beside verse 12, where the Cretans' own poet calls them ψεῦσται, ‘liars’: on an island known for lies stands a God who cannot lie. πρὸ χρόνων αἰωνίων, “before time began” — χρόνος from the time family: time as duration, stretching back through the ages. Before any of it, God had promised life.","words":[{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G893","hebrew":"ἀψευδής","translit":"apseudḗs","english":"can’t lie"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promised"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"}]},{"v":3,"text":"but in his own time revealed his word in the message with which I was entrusted according to the commandment of God our Savior;","understanding":"ἐφανέρωσεν δὲ καιροῖς ἰδίοις τὸν λόγον αὐτοῦ, “but in his own time revealed his word.” Now a different time word: καιρός, the fitting moment, set beside χρόνος in verse 2. God promised across the long χρόνοι; He revealed at the right καιρός. φανερόω from the appear family: to bring into the open what had been hidden. ἐν κηρύγματι ὃ ἐπιστεύθην ἐγώ, “in the message with which I was entrusted” — κήρυγμα, a herald's proclamation. κατ᾽ ἐπιταγὴν τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν θεοῦ, “according to the commandment of God our Savior.” In Titus ‘Savior’ passes between the Father and the Son three times (here and verse 4; 2:10 and 2:13; 3:4 and 3:6).","words":[{"strongs":"G2398","hebrew":"ἴδιος","translit":"ídios","english":"own"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2782","hebrew":"κήρυγμα","translit":"kḗrygma","english":"message"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"entrusted"},{"strongs":"G2003","hebrew":"ἐπιταγή","translit":"epitagḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":4,"text":"to Titus, my true child according to a common faith: Grace, mercy, and peace from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ our Savior.","understanding":"Τίτῳ γνησίῳ τέκνῳ κατὰ κοινὴν πίστιν, “to Titus, my true child according to a common faith.” γνήσιος means legitimate, genuine — the same word Paul used of Timothy (1 Timothy 1:2). Titus was a Greek and Paul a Jew, yet they shared a ‘common’ (κοινός) faith: one gospel for Jew and Gentile. χάρις, ἔλεος, εἰρήνη, “Grace, mercy, and peace” — the WEB follows manuscripts that include ‘mercy’; many of the oldest read only ‘grace and peace’. Then the title moves: ἀπὸ θεοῦ πατρὸς καὶ κυρίου Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν, “from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ our Savior.” In verse 3 God is Savior; here Christ is.","words":[{"strongs":"G5103","hebrew":"Τίτος","translit":"Títos","english":"Titus"},{"strongs":"G1103","hebrew":"γνήσιος","translit":"gnḗsios","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G2839","hebrew":"κοινός","translit":"koinós","english":"common"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":5,"text":"I left you in Crete for this reason, that you would set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city, as I directed you;","understanding":"τούτου χάριν κατέλιπόν σε ἐν Κρήτῃ, “I left you in Crete for this reason,” ἵνα τὰ λείποντα ἐπιδιορθώσῃ, “that you would set in order the things that were lacking” — ἐπιδιορθόω, to straighten further, to set right what remains crooked (διορθόω was used of setting a broken bone). The churches existed, but they were unfinished. καὶ καταστήσῃς κατὰ πόλιν πρεσβυτέρους, “and appoint elders in every city” — plural: a team of elders in each town, as Paul had done in Galatia (Acts 14:23). ὡς ἐγώ σοι διεταξάμην, “as I directed you.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2641","hebrew":"καταλείπω","translit":"kataleípō","english":"left"},{"strongs":"G2914","hebrew":"Κρήτη","translit":"Krḗtē","english":"Crete"},{"strongs":"G3007","hebrew":"λείπω","translit":"leípō","english":"lacking"},{"strongs":"G1930","hebrew":"ἐπιδιορθόω","translit":"epidiorthóō","english":"set in order"},{"strongs":"G2525","hebrew":"καθίστημι","translit":"kathístēmi","english":"appoint"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G1299","hebrew":"διατάσσω","translit":"diatássō","english":"directed"}]},{"v":6,"text":"if anyone is blameless, the husband of one wife, having children who believe, who are not accused of loose or unruly behavior.","understanding":"εἴ τίς ἐστιν ἀνέγκλητος, “if anyone is blameless” — not sinless, but with no charge that sticks: unaccused. μιᾶς γυναικὸς ἀνήρ, “the husband of one wife” — literally ‘a one-woman man’, as in 1 Timothy 3:2: faithful in marriage. τέκνα ἔχων πιστά, “having children who believe” — πιστός can mean ‘believing’ or ‘faithful, trustworthy’; the WEB takes the first, others read ‘children who are faithful’, that is, well-ordered. Either way, the point is the next phrase: μὴ ἐν κατηγορίᾳ ἀσωτίας ἢ ἀνυπότακτα, “who are not accused of loose or unruly behavior.” A man's home is the first test of his leadership.","words":[{"strongs":"G410","hebrew":"ἀνέγκλητος","translit":"anénklētos","english":"blameless"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husband"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G2724","hebrew":"κατηγορία","translit":"katēgoría","english":"accused"},{"strongs":"G810","hebrew":"ἀσωτία","translit":"asōtía","english":"loose"},{"strongs":"G506","hebrew":"ἀνυπότακτος","translit":"anypótaktos","english":"unruly"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For the overseer must be blameless, as God’s steward; not self-pleasing, not easily angered, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy for dishonest gain;","understanding":"δεῖ γὰρ τὸν ἐπίσκοπον ἀνέγκλητον εἶναι, “For the overseer must be blameless.” Notice that the ‘elders’ of verse 5 are now called ‘overseer’: two names for the same office — elder for maturity, overseer for the task. ὡς θεοῦ οἰκονόμον, “as God’s steward” — the manager of a household that belongs to someone else. Then five ‘nots’: μὴ αὐθάδη, “not self-pleasing” — stubbornly having his own way; μὴ ὀργίλον, “not easily angered”; μὴ πάροινον, “not given to wine”; μὴ πλήκτην, “not violent” — not a striker; μὴ αἰσχροκερδῆ, “not greedy for dishonest gain.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1985","hebrew":"ἐπίσκοπος","translit":"epískopos","english":"overseer"},{"strongs":"G410","hebrew":"ἀνέγκλητος","translit":"anénklētos","english":"blameless"},{"strongs":"G3623","hebrew":"οἰκονόμος","translit":"oikonómos","english":"steward"},{"strongs":"G829","hebrew":"αὐθάδης","translit":"authádēs","english":"self-pleasing"},{"strongs":"G3711","hebrew":"ὀργίλος","translit":"orgílos","english":"easily angered"},{"strongs":"G3943","hebrew":"πάροινος","translit":"pároinos","english":"given to wine"},{"strongs":"G4131","hebrew":"πλήκτης","translit":"plḗktēs","english":"violent"},{"strongs":"G146","hebrew":"αἰσχροκερδής","translit":"aischrokerdḗs","english":"greedy for dishonest gain"}]},{"v":8,"text":"but given to hospitality, a lover of good, sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled;","understanding":"Now six positives. Two come from the love family: φιλόξενον, “given to hospitality” — a lover of strangers, opening his home; φιλάγαθον, “a lover of good” — loving good things and good people (the exact opposite of the ἀφιλάγαθοι of 2 Timothy 3:3). Then two from the new self-control family: σώφρονα, “sober minded” — σώφρων, a sound, sensible mind; and ἐγκρατῆ, “self-controlled” — ἐγκρατής, strength held over one's own desires. The first is about how a man thinks; the second about how he governs his appetites. Between them: δίκαιον, “fair” — just toward people; ὅσιον, “holy” — devout toward God.","words":[{"strongs":"G5382","hebrew":"φιλόξενος","translit":"philóxenos","english":"hospitality"},{"strongs":"G5358","hebrew":"φιλάγαθος","translit":"philágathos","english":"lover of good"},{"strongs":"G4998","hebrew":"σώφρων","translit":"sṓphrōn","english":"sober minded"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"fair"},{"strongs":"G3741","hebrew":"ὅσιος","translit":"hósios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1468","hebrew":"ἐγκρατής","translit":"enkratḗs","english":"self-controlled"}]},{"v":9,"text":"holding to the faithful word which is according to the teaching, that he may be able to exhort in the sound doctrine, and to convict those who contradict him.","understanding":"ἀντεχόμενον τοῦ κατὰ τὴν διδαχὴν πιστοῦ λόγου, “holding to the faithful word which is according to the teaching” — ἀντέχομαι, clinging firmly, as to a rope. An elder is not an innovator but a guardian of the apostolic word. Its purpose is twofold: ἵνα δυνατὸς ᾖ καὶ παρακαλεῖν ἐν τῇ διδασκαλίᾳ τῇ ὑγιαινούσῃ, “that he may be able to exhort in the sound doctrine” — ὑγιαίνω, healthy, the letter's recurring word — καὶ τοὺς ἀντιλέγοντας ἐλέγχειν, “and to convict those who contradict him.” To encourage the flock and to refute error: feeding and protecting.","words":[{"strongs":"G472","hebrew":"ἀντέχομαι","translit":"antéchomai","english":"holding"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G1415","hebrew":"δυνατός","translit":"dynatós","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"convict"},{"strongs":"G483","hebrew":"ἀντιλέγω","translit":"antilégō","english":"contradict"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For there are also many unruly men, vain talkers and deceivers, especially those of the circumcision,","understanding":"εἰσὶν γὰρ πολλοὶ ἀνυπότακτοι, “For there are also many unruly men” — the same word as the elders' children in verse 6: refusing to be under order. ματαιολόγοι, “vain talkers” — empty speech, words that lead nowhere; καὶ φρεναπάται, “and deceivers” — literally ‘mind-deceivers’. μάλιστα οἱ ἐκ τῆς περιτομῆς, “especially those of the circumcision” — Jewish believers who insisted on the law's rituals (compare the ‘Jewish fables’ and food rules of verses 14–15), the same party Paul had resisted when Titus himself was pressed to be circumcised.","words":[{"strongs":"G4183","hebrew":"πολύς","translit":"polýs","english":"many"},{"strongs":"G506","hebrew":"ἀνυπότακτος","translit":"anypótaktos","english":"unruly"},{"strongs":"G3151","hebrew":"ματαιολόγος","translit":"mataiológos","english":"vain talkers"},{"strongs":"G5423","hebrew":"φρεναπάτης","translit":"phrenapátēs","english":"deceivers"},{"strongs":"G4061","hebrew":"περιτομή","translit":"peritomḗ","english":"circumcision"}]},{"v":11,"text":"whose mouths must be stopped; men who overthrow whole houses, teaching things which they ought not, for dishonest gain’s sake.","understanding":"οὓς δεῖ ἐπιστομίζειν, “whose mouths must be stopped” — ἐπιστομίζω, to put something over the mouth, to muzzle. Not by force, but by the elders' sound teaching and refutation (v. 9). οἵτινες ὅλους οἴκους ἀνατρέπουσιν, “men who overthrow whole houses” — whole families, and the house churches that met in them. διδάσκοντες ἃ μὴ δεῖ αἰσχροῦ κέρδους χάριν, “teaching things which they ought not, for dishonest gain’s sake.” False teaching and greed travel together.","words":[{"strongs":"G1993","hebrew":"ἐπιστομίζω","translit":"epistomízō","english":"mouths must be stopped"},{"strongs":"G3650","hebrew":"ὅλος","translit":"hólos","english":"whole"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"houses"},{"strongs":"G396","hebrew":"ἀνατρέπω","translit":"anatrépō","english":"overthrow"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G150","hebrew":"αἰσχρός","translit":"aischrós","english":"dishonest"},{"strongs":"G2771","hebrew":"κέρδος","translit":"kérdos","english":"gain"}]},{"v":12,"text":"One of them, a prophet of their own, said, “Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, and idle gluttons.”","understanding":"εἶπέν τις ἐξ αὐτῶν, ἴδιος αὐτῶν προφήτης, “One of them, a prophet of their own, said” — the Cretan poet Epimenides (about 600 BC), whom the Cretans honoured as a seer. Paul quotes pagan poets elsewhere too (Acts 17:28). The line: “Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, and idle gluttons.” ψεῦσται, ‘liars’ — the Greeks even coined a verb, ‘to Cretanize’, meaning to lie; κακὰ θηρία, ‘evil beasts’ — κακός from the evil family: brutish and harmful; γαστέρες ἀργαί, ‘idle bellies’ — ἀργός, lazy. Lies, brutality and laziness: the exact opposites of the letter's truth, gentleness and good works. And against ‘always liars’ stands ‘God, who can’t lie’ (v. 2).","words":[{"strongs":"G2912","hebrew":"Κρής","translit":"Krḗs","english":"Cretans"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophet"},{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liars"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beasts"},{"strongs":"G692","hebrew":"ἀργός","translit":"argós","english":"idle"},{"strongs":"G1064","hebrew":"γαστήρ","translit":"gastḗr","english":"gluttons"}]},{"v":13,"text":"This testimony is true. For this cause, reprove them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith,","understanding":"ἡ μαρτυρία αὕτη ἐστὶν ἀληθής, “This testimony is true.” Paul is not condemning every individual Cretan — he has just told Titus to appoint Cretan elders of fine character. He is saying that the proverb fits what the false teachers are doing. δι᾽ ἣν αἰτίαν ἔλεγχε αὐτοὺς ἀποτόμως, “For this cause, reprove them sharply” — ἀποτόμως, cutting, decisive. But the goal is healing: ἵνα ὑγιαίνωσιν ἐν τῇ πίστει, “that they may be sound in the faith” — ὑγιαίνω again: healthy. Sharp rebuke is surgery, not cruelty.","words":[{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G227","hebrew":"ἀληθής","translit":"alēthḗs","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G156","hebrew":"αἰτία","translit":"aitía","english":"cause"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reprove"},{"strongs":"G664","hebrew":"ἀποτόμως","translit":"apotómōs","english":"sharply"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":14,"text":"not paying attention to Jewish fables and commandments of men who turn away from the truth.","understanding":"μὴ προσέχοντες Ἰουδαϊκοῖς μύθοις, “not paying attention to Jewish fables” — μῦθος, invented stories, as in 1 Timothy 1:4 and 2 Timothy 4:4 — καὶ ἐντολαῖς ἀνθρώπων, “and commandments of men” — exactly the charge Jesus brought against the Pharisees, quoting Isaiah: “teaching as doctrines the commandments of men” (Mark 7:7). ἀποστρεφομένων τὴν ἀλήθειαν, “who turn away from the truth.” Human rules dressed as divine commands always pull people away from the truth.","words":[{"strongs":"G4337","hebrew":"προσέχω","translit":"proséchō","english":"paying attention"},{"strongs":"G2451","hebrew":"Ἰουδαϊκός","translit":"Ioudaïkós","english":"Jewish"},{"strongs":"G3454","hebrew":"μῦθος","translit":"mŷthos","english":"fables"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G654","hebrew":"ἀποστρέφω","translit":"apostréphō","english":"turn away"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":15,"text":"To the pure, all things are pure; but to those who are defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure; but both their mind and their conscience are defiled.","understanding":"πάντα μὲν καθαρὰ τοῖς καθαροῖς, “To the pure, all things are pure.” The context is food and ritual purity rules (the ‘commandments of men’ of v. 14). It is not permission to sin, but freedom from man-made taboos about God's good creation. τοῖς δὲ μεμιαμμένοις καὶ ἀπίστοις οὐδὲν καθαρόν, “but to those who are defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure” — μιαίνω, stained. ἀλλὰ μεμίανται αὐτῶν καὶ ὁ νοῦς καὶ ἡ συνείδησις, “but both their mind and their conscience are defiled.” Impurity is not in things but in the heart, as Jesus taught.","words":[{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G3392","hebrew":"μιαίνω","translit":"miaínō","english":"defiled"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G3762","hebrew":"οὐδείς","translit":"oudeís","english":"nothing"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"}]},{"v":16,"text":"They profess that they know God, but by their deeds they deny him, being abominable, disobedient, and unfit for any good work.","understanding":"θεὸν ὁμολογοῦσιν εἰδέναι, “They profess that they know God” — εἰδέναι, from οἶδα in the know family: they claim to know. But verse 1 spoke of ἐπίγνωσις, knowledge that leads to godliness; theirs leads nowhere. τοῖς δὲ ἔργοις ἀρνοῦνται, “but by their deeds they deny him” — ἔργον from the work family. Their lips say yes; their lives say no. βδελυκτοὶ ὄντες καὶ ἀπειθεῖς, “being abominable, disobedient,” καὶ πρὸς πᾶν ἔργον ἀγαθὸν ἀδόκιμοι, “and unfit for any good work” — ἀγαθός from the good family; ἀδόκιμος, failed the test. Compare the man of God equipped ‘for every good work’ (2 Timothy 3:17). The rest of the letter shows what a people ‘fit for good works’ looks like.","words":[{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"profess"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"deny"},{"strongs":"G947","hebrew":"βδελυκτός","translit":"bdelyktós","english":"abominable"},{"strongs":"G545","hebrew":"ἀπειθής","translit":"apeithḗs","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G96","hebrew":"ἀδόκιμος","translit":"adókimos","english":"unfit"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The God Who Cannot Lie","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a harbour town on Crete at night. In the market, traders swear false oaths, merchants rig their scales, and everyone expects to be cheated. Lies are so common that the island's name has become a byword for them. Then picture, high above the town, a lighthouse that has never once failed. Ships have trusted its light through every storm for generations. It does not flicker. It does not deceive. It simply shines.","meaning":"Paul writes of “eternal life, which God, who can’t lie, promised before time began” (v. 2). Among people who were “always liars” (v. 12), the gospel rests on a God whose word is utterly reliable. He promised before time, and “in his own time revealed his word” (v. 3). Whatever has deceived you, God's promise of eternal life in Christ will not. Build your life on the light that does not fail."},{"title":"Setting the Bone","verseRef":"v. 5-9","image":"Picture a doctor called to a village where a man has broken his leg. Someone has bandaged it, but the bone has not been set straight, and the man limps badly. The doctor gently but firmly realigns the bone, splints it, and then shows the family how to care for it so it heals strong. He does not amputate; he restores. He leaves trusted people in place to watch over the healing.","meaning":"Paul left Titus in Crete “that you would set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city” (v. 5). The word means to straighten further, like setting a bone. The churches existed but were not yet whole. The remedy was godly elders: “sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled” (v. 8), “holding to the faithful word” (v. 9), able to encourage and correct. Ask the Spirit what in your life or church still needs to be set straight."},{"title":"The Lips and the Hands","verseRef":"v. 15-16","image":"Picture a man standing in a crowded square, holding up a sign that reads ‘I know God’. He speaks loudly about religion. But as you watch, you see his hands: pocketing a coin that is not his, shoving a beggar aside, gesturing angrily at his servant. The sign says one thing; the hands say another. Then picture a quiet woman nearby who has said nothing about God, but whose hands are busy feeding, comforting and helping.","meaning":"“They profess that they know God, but by their deeds they deny him” (v. 16). Knowing God is not proven by claims but by a life that fits the truth — “the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (v. 1). Ask the Spirit: do my hands agree with my lips? Is my profession of faith matched by good works?"}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting: Truth and Godliness (v. 1-4)","body":"Paul, God's servant and Christ's apostle, writes for the faith of God's chosen and the knowledge of the truth that leads to godliness, in hope of eternal life promised by the God who cannot lie and now revealed in the preaching entrusted to him; he greets Titus, his true child in a common faith."},{"label":"Appointing Elders (v. 5-9)","body":"Titus is to complete the order of the Cretan churches by appointing elders in every city: men of blameless family life and character, God's stewards, free of arrogance, anger, drunkenness, violence and greed, and marked by hospitality, love of good, sound-mindedness, justice, holiness and self-control, holding firmly to the trustworthy word so they can encourage and refute."},{"label":"Silencing False Teachers (v. 10-16)","body":"Many unruly, empty-talking deceivers, especially from the circumcision party, are upsetting households for gain; like the Cretan proverb about liars and lazy gluttons, they must be sharply rebuked so that they become sound in faith, leaving Jewish myths and human rules; they profess to know God but deny Him by their deeds."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Truth That Leads to Godliness","paragraphs":["Paul's greeting packs the whole letter into a few lines. He is “a servant of God, and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of God’s chosen ones, and the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (v. 1). That last phrase sets the agenda. Truth is not merely to be believed; it produces a way of life. The false teachers on Crete had knowledge without godliness. Titus must build churches where knowing the truth and living godly lives go together.","The foundation is God's promise: “in hope of eternal life, which God, who can’t lie, promised before time began” (v. 2). Two time words show the shape of God's plan. Before the long ages of χρόνος, God promised. Then “in his own time” (καιρός) He “revealed his word in the message with which I was entrusted” (v. 3). Paul had said the same to the Galatians: “But when the fullness of the time came, God sent out his Son” (Galatians 4:4). God's promises are not hurried and not late.","And this God “can’t lie” (v. 2). Hebrews says it is “impossible for God to lie” (Hebrews 6:18). The contrast with Crete is deliberate. The island's own prophet said, “Cretans are always liars” (v. 12). Into a culture of deceit comes a gospel from a God whose every word is true. That is why the church must be a community of truth: its message rests on the faithfulness of the One who sent it."]},{"heading":"Elders: Stewards of God's Household","paragraphs":["Paul left Titus in Crete “that you would set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city” (v. 5). The churches needed leaders. The qualities Paul lists closely match 1 Timothy 3, and the names ‘elder’ (v. 5) and ‘overseer’ (v. 7) are used for the same people, as Paul did with the Ephesian elders, telling them that the Holy Spirit “has made you overseers, to shepherd the assembly” (Acts 20:28).","The overseer must be “blameless, as God’s steward” (v. 7). A steward manages what belongs to someone else. So the elder's character matters more than his gifts: not arrogant, quick-tempered, a drinker, violent or greedy, but “given to hospitality, a lover of good, sober minded, fair, holy, self-controlled” (v. 8). Two love-family words (lover of strangers, lover of good) and two self-control words (a sound mind, mastery over desire) sum up the man. His home must reflect the same order: faithful to his wife, with children not marked by wild or rebellious living (v. 6).","And he must be a man of the word: “holding to the faithful word which is according to the teaching, that he may be able to exhort in the sound doctrine, and to convict those who contradict him” (v. 9). The elder feeds and protects. He encourages God's people with healthy teaching, and he answers those who oppose it. Without such leaders, a church is open to every wind of error."]},{"heading":"Lips That Profess, Lives That Deny","paragraphs":["The need for elders was urgent, because “there are also many unruly men, vain talkers and deceivers, especially those of the circumcision” (v. 10). These teachers were upsetting “whole houses, teaching things which they ought not, for dishonest gain’s sake” (v. 11). Like the false brothers Paul had resisted in Galatia, they wanted to add the law's rituals to the gospel. Earlier, at Antioch, men had taught, “Unless you are circumcised after the custom of Moses, you can’t be saved” (Acts 15:1). Titus himself had been a test case of that controversy.","Paul's response is direct: “reprove them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith” (v. 13). The goal is not to win an argument but to heal the sick. They must stop “paying attention to Jewish fables and commandments of men who turn away from the truth” (v. 14). Then comes a principle about purity: “To the pure, all things are pure; but to those who are defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure” (v. 15). Jesus taught the same: “There is nothing from outside of the man, that going into him can defile him; but the things which proceed out of the man are those that defile the man” (Mark 7:15).","The chapter ends with a devastating verdict: “They profess that they know God, but by their deeds they deny him, being abominable, disobedient, and unfit for any good work” (v. 16). This is the opposite of verse 1. True knowledge leads to godliness; false knowledge leads to deeds that deny God. The rest of the letter will describe a people who adorn the gospel with good works. Jesus gave the test: “Therefore by their fruits you will know them” (Matthew 7:20)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Faithful Stewards and the Test of Fruit","paragraphs":["Paul's pattern of appointing elders goes back to his first missionary journey: “When they had appointed elders for them in every assembly, and had prayed with fasting, they commended them to the Lord, on whom they had believed” (Acts 14:23). Peter gives the same charge to elders: “Shepherd the flock of God which is among you, exercising the oversight, not under compulsion, but voluntarily, not for dishonest gain, but willingly” (1 Peter 5:2). Leaders are stewards, and “it is required of stewards, that they be found faithful” (1 Corinthians 4:2).","The false teachers' focus on rules echoes what Jesus condemned in the Pharisees: “But they worship me in vain, teaching as doctrines the commandments of men” (Mark 7:7). Paul wrote similarly to the Colossians about regulations “according to the precepts and doctrines of men” (Colossians 2:22). And he affirmed to Timothy that “every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be rejected, if it is received with thanksgiving” (1 Timothy 4:4). Purity is a matter of the heart, not of rules about food.","John states the test of 1:16 plainly: the one who claims ‘I know him’ “and doesn’t keep his commandments, is a liar, and the truth isn’t in him” (1 John 2:4). The devil, Jesus said, “is a liar, and the father of lies” (John 8:44). But the God of the gospel cannot lie, and those who truly know Him become people of truth. As Paul quoted a Greek poet in Athens, he quotes a Cretan one here: the gospel is not afraid to speak to a culture in its own words, and then to call it to something better."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Who cannot lie / truthful","hebrew":"ἀψευδής","translit":"apseudḗs","strongs":"G893","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “veracious.” Its only New Testament use is here, of God Himself: ὁ ἀψευδὴς θεός, ‘the un-lying God’. The word is formed by putting the negative ἀ- in front of the root of ψεῦδος, a lie — the same root as ψεῦσται, the ‘liars’ of verse 12. Paul sets the God who cannot lie against an island famous for lies. Every promise of the gospel rests on this attribute."},{"english":"Set in order / straighten further","hebrew":"ἐπιδιορθόω","translit":"epidiorthóō","strongs":"G1930","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “to straighten further, i.e. (figuratively) arrange additionally.” It is formed from ἐπί, further, and διορθόω, to make straight — a word used by Greek doctors for setting broken limbs. Paul had begun the work; Titus must finish it. Churches, like bones, can exist without being properly aligned. Godly leadership and sound teaching are God's means of setting them straight."},{"english":"Steward / household manager","hebrew":"οἰκονόμος","translit":"oikonómos","strongs":"G3623","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “a house-distributor (i.e. manager), or overseer, i.e. an employee in that capacity; by extension, a fiscal agent (treasurer); figuratively, a preacher (of the Gospel).” A steward ran a household on behalf of its owner: he had authority, but it was delegated, and he was accountable. Calling the overseer ‘God's steward’ means the church is God's house, not the elder's. He serves the Master's interests, not his own."},{"english":"Sober-minded / of sound mind","hebrew":"σώφρων","translit":"sṓphrōn","strongs":"G4998","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “safe (sound) in mind, i.e. self-controlled (moderate as to opinion or passion).” It comes from σῴζω, to save, and φρήν, the mind: a mind kept safe and whole. It is the key quality of Titus 2, where the related words are applied to old and young, men and women. It is not gloominess but sanity — seeing God, oneself and the world as they really are, and living accordingly."},{"english":"Deceiver / mind-deceiver","hebrew":"φρεναπάτης","translit":"phrenapátēs","strongs":"G5423","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “a mind-misleader, i.e. seducer.” It joins φρήν, the mind, and ἀπατάω, to deceive. It is the dark mirror of σώφρων: where the elder has a sound mind, the false teacher leads minds astray. Paul's concern is not only what people do but what they think, because deception of the mind leads to the ruin of whole households."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"Through this chapter the Holy Spirit speaks to you about trusting God's word, about the character of leadership, and about whether your life matches your profession.","items":[{"title":"His Word Will Not Fail You","body":"You may have been deceived by people, systems or even your own heart. The Spirit wants to anchor you in the God who cannot lie. His promise of eternal life was made before time and revealed at the right time in Christ. Ask Him to strengthen your trust in His promises where disappointment has made you doubtful."},{"title":"Character Before Gifts","body":"God cares more about who His leaders are than about what they can do. Whether or not you lead, the Spirit is forming in you hospitality, love of good, a sound mind, fairness, holiness and self-control. Ask Him to show you which of these He is working on now, and yield to His training."},{"title":"Let Your Deeds Confess Him","body":"It is possible to profess God with your lips and deny Him with your deeds. The Spirit lovingly exposes any gap between what you say and how you live. Invite Him to search you. Where He shows you a contradiction, repent and ask Him to make your life a true confession of Christ."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Truth Check","body":"Is there any area where you shade the truth — in business, conversation or social media? Ask God to make you a person of truth, like the God who cannot lie (v. 2)."},{"title":"The Character Check","body":"Read verses 6–8 slowly. Mark one quality you are strong in and one where you are weak. Pray about the weak one and choose one concrete step to grow in it this week."},{"title":"The Deeds Check","body":"Write down what you say you believe about God. Next to it, write how your actions this week showed or contradicted it (v. 16)."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Pray for Your Leaders by the List","body":"Use verses 6–9 as a prayer list for the elders or leaders of your church. Each day for a week, pray one quality over them by name: blameless family life, freedom from arrogance and anger, hospitality, love of good, sound-mindedness, justice, holiness, self-control and faithfulness to the word. Write a short note of encouragement to one of them at the end of the week."},{"title":"Practise Hospitality as Love","body":"The elder must be φιλόξενος, a lover of strangers. This month, invite someone outside your usual circle — a newcomer, a lonely person, someone from another background — to share a meal in your home or elsewhere. Listen to their story. Let your hospitality be a small picture of God's welcome."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, You are the God who cannot lie. Before time began You promised eternal life, and in Your own time You revealed it in Jesus Christ through the preaching of the gospel. Thank You that I can build my life on Your word. Give me a love for the knowledge of the truth that leads to godliness. Set in order what is still lacking in my life and in my church, as a doctor sets a broken bone. Raise up leaders who are blameless, faithful in their homes, not self-willed, angry, given to drink, violent or greedy, but hospitable, lovers of good, sound-minded, fair, holy and self-controlled, holding firmly to Your faithful word. Make me that kind of person too. Protect Your people from empty talkers and deceivers who upset households for gain. Keep me from human rules that pretend to be Your commands. Purify my mind and conscience. Let my deeds never deny what my lips confess, and make me fit for every good work. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Titus at the Jerusalem Council Debate","ref":"Galatians 2:1-5; Acts 15:1-2","relatedVerses":"v. 4, 10, 14","summary":"Fourteen years after his conversion, Paul went up to Jerusalem, “taking Titus also with me” (Galatians 2:1). Some were insisting that Gentile believers must be circumcised, as men from Judea had taught at Antioch: “Unless you are circumcised after the custom of Moses, you can’t be saved” (Acts 15:1). Titus, an uncircumcised Greek believer, became the test case. Paul refused to give way: “But not even Titus, who was with me, being a Greek, was compelled to be circumcised” (Galatians 2:3). Years later, Titus faced on Crete the same kind of teachers, ‘those of the circumcision’ (Titus 1:10). He knew from his own story that the gospel is received by faith alone, and that a ‘common faith’ unites Jew and Gentile."},{"title":"Jesus and the Traditions of Men","ref":"Mark 7:1-23","relatedVerses":"v. 14-15","summary":"The Pharisees criticised Jesus' disciples for eating without ceremonially washing their hands. Jesus answered from Isaiah that they worshipped in vain, “teaching as doctrines the commandments of men” (Mark 7:7). Then He called the crowd and taught a revolutionary principle: “There is nothing from outside of the man, that going into him can defile him; but the things which proceed out of the man are those that defile the man” (Mark 7:15). Mark notes that by this Jesus declared all foods clean. Evil thoughts, greed, deceit and pride come from the heart. Paul applies the same principle on Crete: “To the pure, all things are pure” (Titus 1:15)."},{"title":"Paul Quotes the Poets in Athens","ref":"Acts 17:16-34","relatedVerses":"v. 12-13","summary":"In Athens, Paul spoke to philosophers on the Areopagus. He started from their own altar to an unknown god and then quoted their own writers: ‘In him we live, and move, and have our being’, and “As some of your own poets have said, ‘For we are also his offspring.’” (Acts 17:28). He used their culture's own voices to point them to the true God and to call them to repentance. In Titus 1:12 he does the same with a Cretan poet, letting the island's own prophet describe its besetting sins. The gospel is not afraid to engage a culture in its own words — and then to call it to something far better."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Titus","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Lives That Adorn the Gospel","themeTitle":"“The Grace of God Has Appeared”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"But say the things which fit sound doctrine,","understanding":"σὺ δὲ λάλει ἃ πρέπει τῇ ὑγιαινούσῃ διδασκαλίᾳ, “But say the things which fit sound doctrine.” The ‘but you’ contrasts Titus with the empty talkers of chapter 1. πρέπω means to be fitting, to suit — like clothes that suit the wearer. ὑγιαίνω, ‘sound’, is healthy. Titus is not only to teach correct doctrine but to describe the kind of life that fits it. What follows is not a list of rules but a portrait of healthy believers in every stage of life.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"say"},{"strongs":"G4241","hebrew":"πρέπω","translit":"prépō","english":"fit"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"}]},{"v":2,"text":"that older men should be temperate, sensible, sober minded, sound in faith, in love, and in perseverance:","understanding":"πρεσβύτας νηφαλίους εἶναι, “that older men should be temperate” — νηφάλιος from the self-control family: clear-headed, not clouded by wine. σεμνούς, “sensible” — dignified, worthy of respect. σώφρονας, “sober minded” — σώφρων, a second member of the same family: a sound, sensible mind. Two kinds of soberness: free from intoxication and free from foolishness. Then: ὑγιαίνοντας τῇ πίστει, τῇ ἀγάπῃ, τῇ ὑπομονῇ, “sound in faith, in love, and in perseverance” — healthy in the great trio, with endurance in place of hope, because old age needs staying power.","words":[{"strongs":"G4246","hebrew":"πρεσβύτης","translit":"presbýtēs","english":"older men"},{"strongs":"G3524","hebrew":"νηφάλεος","translit":"nēpháleos","english":"temperate"},{"strongs":"G4586","hebrew":"σεμνός","translit":"semnós","english":"sensible"},{"strongs":"G4998","hebrew":"σώφρων","translit":"sṓphrōn","english":"sober minded"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"}]},{"v":3,"text":"and that older women likewise be reverent in behavior, not slanderers nor enslaved to much wine, teachers of that which is good;","understanding":"πρεσβύτιδας ὡσαύτως ἐν καταστήματι ἱεροπρεπεῖς, “and that older women likewise be reverent in behavior” — ἱεροπρεπής, ‘fitting for a temple’: their whole demeanour is priestly, as if daily life were worship. μὴ διαβόλους, “not slanderers” — διάβολος, the devil's own name; μὴ οἴνῳ πολλῷ δεδουλωμένας, “nor enslaved to much wine” — δουλόω, from the servant family: to be made a slave. Believers are slaves of God (1:1), not of wine. καλοδιδασκάλους, “teachers of that which is good” — καλός from the good family: teachers of what is beautiful and right, by word and example.","words":[{"strongs":"G4247","hebrew":"πρεσβῦτις","translit":"presbŷtis","english":"older women"},{"strongs":"G2412","hebrew":"ἱεροπρεπής","translit":"hieroprepḗs","english":"reverent"},{"strongs":"G2688","hebrew":"κατάστημα","translit":"katástēma","english":"behavior"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"slanderers"},{"strongs":"G1402","hebrew":"δουλόω","translit":"doulóō","english":"enslaved"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G2567","hebrew":"καλοδιδάσκαλος","translit":"kalodidáskalos","english":"teachers of that which is good"}]},{"v":4,"text":"that they may train the young wives to love their husbands, to love their children,","understanding":"ἵνα σωφρονίζωσιν τὰς νέας, “that they may train the young wives” — σωφρονίζω from the self-control family: to bring someone to a sound mind, to coach them in good sense. The older women mentor the younger. And the first lessons are love-family words: φιλάνδρους εἶναι, “to love their husbands” — φίλανδρος, husband-loving; φιλοτέκνους, “to love their children” — φιλότεκνος, child-loving. Both are the warm, affectionate φιλ- kind of love. Even natural love needs to be taught and nurtured.","words":[{"strongs":"G4994","hebrew":"σωφρονίζω","translit":"sōphronízō","english":"train"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"young"},{"strongs":"G5362","hebrew":"φίλανδρος","translit":"phílandros","english":"love their husbands"},{"strongs":"G5388","hebrew":"φιλότεκνος","translit":"philóteknos","english":"love their children"}]},{"v":5,"text":"to be sober minded, chaste, workers at home, kind, being in subjection to their own husbands, that God’s word may not be blasphemed.","understanding":"σώφρονας, “to be sober minded” — σώφρων again; ἁγνάς, “chaste” — pure; οἰκουρούς, “workers at home” — caring for the household (the oldest manuscripts read οἰκουργούς, ‘working at home’); ἀγαθάς, “kind” — ἀγαθός from the good family: good-hearted, generous. ὑποτασσομένας τοῖς ἰδίοις ἀνδράσιν, “being in subjection to their own husbands” — ‘their own’: not to men in general. Paul's reason is missionary: ἵνα μὴ ὁ λόγος τοῦ θεοῦ βλασφημῆται, “that God’s word may not be blasphemed.” Paul also commands husbands to love their wives as Christ loved the church (Ephesians 5:25); the call is never one-sided.","words":[{"strongs":"G4998","hebrew":"σώφρων","translit":"sṓphrōn","english":"sober minded"},{"strongs":"G53","hebrew":"ἁγνός","translit":"hagnós","english":"chaste"},{"strongs":"G3626","hebrew":"οἰκουρός","translit":"oikourós","english":"workers at home"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"kind"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blasphemed"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Likewise, exhort the younger men to be sober minded;","understanding":"τοὺς νεωτέρους ὡσαύτως παρακάλει σωφρονεῖν, “Likewise, exhort the younger men to be sober minded” — σωφρονέω, yet another self-control word: to think straight, to be in one's right mind (the healed demoniac of Mark 5:15). For young men Paul gives only one command, because it includes all the others: control yourselves. Every group in this chapter is called to the σώφρων life — older men, younger women, younger men, and all believers (v. 12).","words":[{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"younger men"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G4993","hebrew":"σωφρονέω","translit":"sōphronéō","english":"sober minded"}]},{"v":7,"text":"in all things showing yourself an example of good works; in your teaching showing integrity, seriousness, incorruptibility,","understanding":"περὶ πάντα σεαυτὸν παρεχόμενος τύπον καλῶν ἔργων, “in all things showing yourself an example of good works” — τύπος, a pattern stamped out like a die; καλός from the good family and ἔργον from the work family: beautiful, admirable deeds. Titus, probably young himself, must model what he teaches. ἐν τῇ διδασκαλίᾳ ἀδιαφθορίαν, σεμνότητα, ἀφθαρσίαν, “in your teaching showing integrity, seriousness, incorruptibility” — teaching that is uncorrupted, dignified and pure in motive. (The last word is in the manuscripts the WEB follows; many older ones omit it.)","words":[{"strongs":"G5179","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G90","hebrew":"ἀδιαφθορία","translit":"adiaphthoría","english":"integrity"},{"strongs":"G4587","hebrew":"σεμνότης","translit":"semnótēs","english":"seriousness"},{"strongs":"G861","hebrew":"ἀφθαρσία","translit":"aphtharsía","english":"incorruptibility"}]},{"v":8,"text":"and soundness of speech that can’t be condemned; that he who opposes you may be ashamed, having no evil thing to say about us.","understanding":"λόγον ὑγιῆ ἀκατάγνωστον, “and soundness of speech that can’t be condemned” — ὑγιής, healthy, the adjective of the letter's favourite word. ἵνα ὁ ἐξ ἐναντίας ἐντραπῇ, “that he who opposes you may be ashamed,” μηδὲν ἔχων λέγειν περὶ ἡμῶν φαῦλον, “having no evil thing to say about us.” φαῦλος is a third member of the evil family, beside κακός and πονηρός: the worthless, shabby kind of wrong. The aim is a life so clean that critics are left with nothing to say.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"speech"},{"strongs":"G5199","hebrew":"ὑγιής","translit":"hygiḗs","english":"soundness"},{"strongs":"G176","hebrew":"ἀκατάγνωστος","translit":"akatágnōstos","english":"can’t be condemned"},{"strongs":"G1727","hebrew":"ἐναντίος","translit":"enantíos","english":"opposes"},{"strongs":"G1788","hebrew":"ἐντρέπω","translit":"entrépō","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G5337","hebrew":"φαῦλος","translit":"phaûlos","english":"evil"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Exhort servants to be in subjection to their own masters, and to be well-pleasing in all things; not contradicting;","understanding":"δούλους ἰδίοις δεσπόταις ὑποτάσσεσθαι, “Exhort servants to be in subjection to their own masters” — δοῦλος from the servant family: slaves, who made up a large part of the early church. Paul does not endorse slavery (he condemns slave-traders in 1 Timothy 1:10 and urges slaves to gain freedom when they can, 1 Corinthians 7:21), but he addresses believers where they are. ἐν πᾶσιν εὐαρέστους εἶναι, “and to be well-pleasing in all things” — the word normally used of pleasing God; μὴ ἀντιλέγοντας, “not contradicting” — not answering back.","words":[{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G2398","hebrew":"ἴδιος","translit":"ídios","english":"own"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"masters"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G2101","hebrew":"εὐάρεστος","translit":"euárestos","english":"well-pleasing"},{"strongs":"G483","hebrew":"ἀντιλέγω","translit":"antilégō","english":"contradicting"}]},{"v":10,"text":"not stealing, but showing all good fidelity; that they may adorn the doctrine of God, our Savior, in all things.","understanding":"μὴ νοσφιζομένους, “not stealing” — pilfering, as Ananias kept back part of the price (Acts 5:2). ἀλλὰ πᾶσαν πίστιν ἐνδεικνυμένους ἀγαθήν, “but showing all good fidelity” — ἀγαθός from the good family; πίστις here is faithfulness, trustworthiness. And the glorious purpose: ἵνα τὴν διδασκαλίαν τὴν τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν θεοῦ κοσμῶσιν ἐν πᾶσιν, “that they may adorn the doctrine of God, our Savior, in all things.” κοσμέω, to put in order, to decorate (our ‘cosmetic’). The lowest members of society can make the gospel beautiful.","words":[{"strongs":"G3557","hebrew":"νοσφίζομαι","translit":"nosphízomai","english":"stealing"},{"strongs":"G1731","hebrew":"ἐνδείκνυμι","translit":"endeíknymi","english":"showing"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"fidelity"},{"strongs":"G2885","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","english":"adorn"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men,","understanding":"Now the reason for everything: ἐπεφάνη γὰρ ἡ χάρις τοῦ θεοῦ, “For the grace of God has appeared” — ἐπιφαίνω from the appear family: to shine forth, as the dawn breaks over the horizon. Grace is not an idea but an event, the coming of Christ. ἡ σωτήριος πᾶσιν ἀνθρώποις, “bringing salvation to all men” — not only to the old or the young, the free or the slave, but to every kind of person in the list just given.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2014","hebrew":"ἐπιφαίνω","translit":"epiphaínō","english":"appeared"},{"strongs":"G4992","hebrew":"σωτήριον","translit":"sōtḗrion","english":"bringing salvation"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"}]},{"v":12,"text":"instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age;","understanding":"παιδεύουσα ἡμᾶς, “instructing us” — παιδεύω, to train a child: grace is a tutor. It teaches a no and a yes. The no: ἀρνησάμενοι τὴν ἀσέβειαν καὶ τὰς κοσμικὰς ἐπιθυμίας, “denying ungodliness and worldly lusts.” The yes: σωφρόνως καὶ δικαίως καὶ εὐσεβῶς ζήσωμεν, “we would live soberly, righteously, and godly.” Two families meet. From the godliness family: ἀσέβεια, irreverence, and εὐσεβῶς, reverently — the very opposites in one verse. From the self-control family: σωφρόνως, ‘soberly’, the chapter's key word. Three directions: toward self (soberly), toward others (righteously), toward God (godly). ἐν τῷ νῦν αἰῶνι, “in this present age” — αἰών from the time and world families: this passing age.","words":[{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"instructing"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"denying"},{"strongs":"G763","hebrew":"ἀσέβεια","translit":"asébeia","english":"ungodliness"},{"strongs":"G2886","hebrew":"κοσμικός","translit":"kosmikós","english":"worldly"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G4996","hebrew":"σωφρόνως","translit":"sōphrónōs","english":"soberly"},{"strongs":"G1346","hebrew":"δικαίως","translit":"dikaíōs","english":"righteously"},{"strongs":"G2153","hebrew":"εὐσεβῶς","translit":"eusebōs","english":"godly"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G3568","hebrew":"νῦν","translit":"nŷn","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"age"}]},{"v":13,"text":"looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ;","understanding":"προσδεχόμενοι τὴν μακαρίαν ἐλπίδα, “looking for the blessed hope” — eagerly waiting to welcome it — καὶ ἐπιφάνειαν τῆς δόξης, “and appearing of the glory.” ἐπιφάνεια from the appear family: the same root as ‘appeared’ in verse 11. The first appearing was grace; the second will be glory. τοῦ μεγάλου θεοῦ καὶ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ.” One Greek article governs both ‘God’ and ‘Savior’, so the most natural reading names Jesus Christ as ‘our great God and Savior’ — as in 2 Peter 1:1. Some translate ‘the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ’; but the grammar, and the fact that ‘appearing’ is always used of Christ, favour the first.","words":[{"strongs":"G4327","hebrew":"προσδέχομαι","translit":"prosdéchomai","english":"looking for"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G2015","hebrew":"ἐπιφάνεια","translit":"epipháneia","english":"appearing"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":14,"text":"who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify for himself a people for his own possession, zealous for good works.","understanding":"ὃς ἔδωκεν ἑαυτὸν ὑπὲρ ἡμῶν, “who gave himself for us” — not merely something, but Himself. Two purposes. First, ἵνα λυτρώσηται ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ πάσης ἀνομίας, “that he might redeem us from all iniquity” — λυτρόω, to set free by paying a ransom; ἀνομία from the sin family: lawlessness, sin as rebellion against God's law. Second, καὶ καθαρίσῃ ἑαυτῷ λαὸν περιούσιον, “and purify for himself a people for his own possession” — περιούσιος echoes God's ‘treasured possession’ language for Israel at Sinai. ζηλωτὴν καλῶν ἔργων, “zealous for good works” — a ‘zealot’ for beautiful deeds (καλός, good family; ἔργον, work family). Redemption from sin always aims at good works.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G3084","hebrew":"λυτρόω","translit":"lytróō","english":"redeem"},{"strongs":"G458","hebrew":"ἀνομία","translit":"anomía","english":"iniquity"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"purify"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G4041","hebrew":"περιούσιος","translit":"perioúsios","english":"own possession"},{"strongs":"G2207","hebrew":"ζηλωτής","translit":"zēlōtḗs","english":"zealous"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Say these things and exhort and reprove with all authority. Let no man despise you.","understanding":"ταῦτα λάλει καὶ παρακάλει καὶ ἔλεγχε, “Say these things and exhort and reprove” — teach, encourage, correct. μετὰ πάσης ἐπιταγῆς, “with all authority.” A word-family point: ‘authority’ here is ἐπιταγή, the authority of a command (the same word as ‘commandment’ in 1:3), not ἐξουσία, the right or power to act. Titus speaks with the weight of God's command behind him. μηδείς σου περιφρονείτω, “Let no man despise you” — περιφρονέω, to think around, to disregard. Compare ‘Let no man despise your youth’ to Timothy.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"Say"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reprove"},{"strongs":"G2003","hebrew":"ἐπιταγή","translit":"epitagḗ","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G4065","hebrew":"περιφρονέω","translit":"periphronéō","english":"despise"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Jewels on the Gospel","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture a plain wooden box containing a priceless gift. On its own, the box draws no attention. Then picture someone setting jewels into its lid — a ruby, an emerald, a pearl — until the box itself shines and everyone wants to see what is inside. Now picture the jewels: a slave who refuses to steal from his master, an older woman gently teaching a young mother, a young man who keeps his temper, a teacher whose life matches his words.","meaning":"Paul wants slaves to show “all good fidelity; that they may adorn the doctrine of God, our Savior, in all things” (v. 10). κοσμέω means to decorate, to set in order. The gospel is beautiful in itself, but the world often sees it first through the lives of believers. Ordinary faithfulness in ordinary places makes the teaching about God attractive. Ask the Spirit: is my life setting jewels on the gospel, or hiding it?"},{"title":"Grace the Tutor","verseRef":"v. 11-12","image":"Picture a wealthy family in the ancient world with a trusted household tutor who walks beside the child every day. He takes the boy's hand when he wanders toward danger and says, ‘No, not that way.’ He points out the right path and says, ‘This way.’ He teaches manners, letters, courage and kindness. Day by day, the child grows to be like the household he belongs to. Now picture that tutor's name: Grace.","meaning":"“For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men, instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (vv. 11–12). Grace does not only forgive; it trains. It teaches us to say no to ungodliness and yes to a sound-minded, upright and godly life. Thank God that His grace is patient with you as a teacher, and ask Him to make you teachable."},{"title":"Two Dawns","verseRef":"v. 11-14","image":"Picture a traveller who has walked through a long, dark night. At last, a thin line of light appears on the eastern horizon: the first dawn. Its light shows him the path and warms him. But it is only the beginning. He walks on toward the east, watching the sky, because he knows that soon the full sun will rise in all its splendour and the whole land will be flooded with light.","meaning":"Paul uses the same word family for both comings of Christ. “For the grace of God has appeared” (v. 11): the first dawn, Christ's coming in grace. And we are “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (v. 13): the full sunrise of His return in glory. Between the two dawns, we walk in the light of grace, “zealous for good works” (v. 14), with our faces toward the coming glory."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Healthy Teaching for Every Age (v. 1-8)","body":"Titus must teach what fits sound doctrine: older men to be clear-headed, dignified, sensible and healthy in faith, love and endurance; older women to be reverent, not slanderers or slaves of wine, teaching what is good and training younger women in love, self-control, purity, care for their homes, kindness and submission to their husbands; younger men to be self-controlled; and Titus himself to model good works and sound, uncorrupted teaching."},{"label":"Slaves Who Adorn the Gospel (v. 9-10)","body":"Believing slaves are to be submissive, pleasing, not answering back or stealing, but showing complete faithfulness, so that they adorn the teaching of God our Saviour in every way."},{"label":"The Grace That Trains (v. 11-15)","body":"The grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation and training us to reject ungodliness and live sensibly, righteously and godly while we wait for the appearing of the glory of our great God and Saviour Jesus Christ, who gave Himself to redeem and purify a people zealous for good works; Titus must teach this with full authority."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Life That Fits the Truth","paragraphs":["After exposing the false teachers, Paul turns to Titus: “But say the things which fit sound doctrine” (v. 1). Healthy teaching produces healthy lives, and Paul describes them group by group. The older men are to be “temperate, sensible, sober minded, sound in faith, in love, and in perseverance” (v. 2). The older women are to be “reverent in behavior, not slanderers nor enslaved to much wine, teachers of that which is good” (v. 3). Age is not a reason to retire from discipleship; it is a calling to model and mentor.","The older women are to train the younger women “to love their husbands, to love their children, to be sober minded, chaste, workers at home, kind, being in subjection to their own husbands, that God’s word may not be blasphemed” (vv. 4–5). Some of these instructions are hard for modern readers. Notice that the reason given is the reputation of the gospel in that culture, that the qualities listed are mostly character, and that elsewhere Paul calls husbands to sacrificial love. The heart of the passage is women discipling women, and families shaped by love.","The younger men receive one comprehensive command: “to be sober minded” (v. 6). Then Titus himself must be the pattern: “in all things showing yourself an example of good works; in your teaching showing integrity, seriousness, incorruptibility, and soundness of speech that can’t be condemned” (vv. 7–8). His life and teaching must leave critics with “no evil thing to say about us” (v. 8). The repeated σώφρων words — temperate, sober minded, train, soberly — show that self-control is not a single virtue for one group but the shape of the whole Christian life."]},{"heading":"Adorning the Teaching","paragraphs":["Paul then addresses slaves, who made up a large part of the early church: “Exhort servants to be in subjection to their own masters, and to be well-pleasing in all things; not contradicting; not stealing, but showing all good fidelity” (vv. 9–10). As in his other letters, he neither ignores their situation nor endorses the system. Elsewhere he condemns slave-traders, tells slaves to take freedom if they can (1 Corinthians 7:21), and commands masters, “give to your servants that which is just and equal, knowing that you also have a Master in heaven” (Colossians 4:1).","The purpose is breathtaking: “that they may adorn the doctrine of God, our Savior, in all things” (v. 10). The word κοσμέω means to arrange beautifully, to decorate. The people with the least power in the ancient world are given the highest calling: to make the teaching about God beautiful by their faithfulness. Jesus said the same about all His followers: “let your light shine before men; that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven” (Matthew 5:16).","Throughout the passage, the gospel's reputation is at stake: “that God’s word may not be blasphemed” (v. 5), that opponents may have “no evil thing to say about us” (v. 8), that believers may “adorn the doctrine” (v. 10). Paul knew the opposite could happen, quoting Scripture to the Romans: “the name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles because of you” (Romans 2:24). Our conduct either decorates or disgraces the gospel."]},{"heading":"The Grace That Trains","paragraphs":["Why should believers live this way? “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men” (v. 11). The ‘for’ makes grace the reason and power for everything above. Grace appeared in the person of Jesus Christ, like the dawn. It brings salvation to all kinds of people — old and young, men and women, slave and free. No group is outside its reach.","And grace trains: “instructing us to the intent that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we would live soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (v. 12). The verb παιδεύω means to train a child. Grace says no to ungodliness and worldly desires, and yes to a life that is sensible toward self, just toward others and devoted toward God. Two word families shape the verse: ungodliness and godliness, and the sober-minded life that runs through the whole chapter.","Grace also points forward: we are “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (v. 13). Christ is called ‘our great God and Savior’ — one of the clearest statements of His deity in the New Testament. He “gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify for himself a people for his own possession, zealous for good works” (v. 14). Redemption has a goal: not only a forgiven people, but a people passionate about doing good. Titus must teach this “with all authority” (v. 15), the authority of God's command."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Grace, Redemption and a People for God's Possession","paragraphs":["Titus 2:11–14 echoes the whole New Testament gospel. Christ “gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us out of this present evil age” (Galatians 1:4). Paul told Timothy that Christ “gave himself as a ransom for all” (1 Timothy 2:6), and Jesus Himself came “to give his life as a ransom for many” (Mark 10:45). Peter writes that believers were redeemed “with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (1 Peter 1:19).","The church is God's treasured people: “But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession” (1 Peter 2:9). Its purpose is good works: “For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God prepared before that we would walk in them” (Ephesians 2:10). And its hope is Christ's return: “For our citizenship is in heaven, from where we also wait for a Savior, the Lord Jesus Christ” (Philippians 3:20). Christ “will appear a second time, without sin, to those who are eagerly waiting for him for salvation” (Hebrews 9:28).","The confession that Jesus is ‘our great God and Savior’ is shared across the New Testament. Peter writes almost the same words: “the righteousness of our God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 1:1). Thomas, seeing the risen Lord, cried, “My Lord and my God!” (John 20:28). And John says that this hope transforms the present: “Everyone who has this hope set on him purifies himself, even as he is pure” (1 John 3:3)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Instruct / train as a child","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","strongs":"G3811","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “to train up a child, i.e. educate, or (by implication), discipline (by punishment).” It comes from παῖς, a child. The word covers the whole of a child's upbringing: teaching, guiding and, when needed, correcting. Hebrews uses it of the Lord's loving discipline. In Titus 2:12 grace itself is the trainer. God's grace does not leave us as we are; it patiently raises us to maturity."},{"english":"Adorn / put in order","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","strongs":"G2885","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “to put in proper order, i.e. decorate (literally or figuratively); specially, to snuff (a wick).” It is related to κόσμος, ordered arrangement, and gives us the word ‘cosmetic’. It is used of decorating the temple with gifts and of a bride adorned for her husband. In Titus 2:10 slaves adorn the teaching of God. Faithful lives arrange the gospel beautifully before watching eyes."},{"english":"Appear / shine forth","hebrew":"ἐπιφαίνω","translit":"epiphaínō","strongs":"G2014","verses":"v. 11","insight":"Strong's: “to shine upon, i.e. become (literally) visible or (figuratively) known.” It is used of stars appearing in the sky and of light dawning on those in darkness. Titus uses it twice of Christ's first coming (2:11; 3:4) and its noun ἐπιφάνεια of His return (2:13). Both comings are a shining-forth of God: first of grace, then of glory. This study tags it in the new appear family."},{"english":"Train to sound-mindedness","hebrew":"σωφρονίζω","translit":"sōphronízō","strongs":"G4994","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to make of sound mind, i.e. (figuratively) to discipline or correct.” It is the verb of the σώφρων family: to help someone become sensible and self-controlled. Older women are to do this for younger women. Sound-mindedness is caught as well as taught — passed on through relationships, example and patient encouragement from those further along the road."},{"english":"One's own special possession","hebrew":"περιούσιος","translit":"perioúsios","strongs":"G4041","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “being beyond usual, i.e. special (one's own).” In the Greek Old Testament it translates the word for God's ‘treasured possession’ when He called Israel at Sinai. Paul applies it to the church that Christ redeemed: a people who belong to Him in a special way. Believers are not only forgiven; they are treasured. And His treasured people are to be zealous for good works."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"Through this chapter the Holy Spirit speaks about your place in God's family, the beauty of ordinary faithfulness, and the training power of grace.","items":[{"title":"Every Season of Life Has a Calling","body":"Whether you are old or young, the Spirit has a calling for you in this season. Older believers are to model and mentor; younger believers are to learn and grow in self-control. Ask Him who you should be learning from, and who you should be investing in. No one is too old to serve or too young to be an example."},{"title":"Your Faithfulness Adorns the Gospel","body":"You may feel that your daily work is ordinary or unseen. The Spirit says it can adorn the teaching about God. Honesty, reliability, kindness and self-control in small things make the gospel beautiful to watching eyes. Ask Him where your conduct can become a jewel on the gospel this week."},{"title":"Let Grace Train You","body":"The grace that saved you is now training you. It says ‘no’ to some things and ‘yes’ to others. Rather than resisting, yield to the Spirit's training. Ask Him what He is teaching you to deny, and what He is teaching you to embrace, as you wait for the appearing of Jesus Christ."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Self-Control Check","body":"Every group in this chapter is called to be σώφρων. Where is your life least sober-minded — in your temper, appetites, spending, screen time or words? Name one area and ask God for grace to train you there (vv. 2, 6, 12)."},{"title":"The Mentoring Check","body":"Who is older in the faith who could mentor you? Who is younger whom you could encourage? Write one name in each column and pray about taking a step this month (vv. 3–4)."},{"title":"The Workplace Check","body":"Think about your work or study. Are you honest, reliable and respectful, so that your conduct adorns the gospel (vv. 9–10)? Identify one way you can show ‘all good fidelity’ this week."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"The Two-Sided Prayer of Grace","body":"Each morning for a month, pray through Titus 2:12. First, the ‘no’: name one ungodly attitude or worldly desire and ask grace to help you deny it today. Then the ‘yes’: choose one way to live soberly (toward yourself), righteously (toward others) and godly (toward God) that day. In the evening, give thanks for how grace trained you."},{"title":"A Zeal for Good Works","body":"Christ redeemed a people ‘zealous for good works’ (v. 14). Plan one good work each week for the next six weeks — practical help for a neighbour, an encouraging visit, a meal, a gift, an act of service at church. Do them not to earn grace but because you have received it, and look for chances to say why."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You that Your grace has appeared, bringing salvation to all people — old and young, men and women, servants and masters. Thank You that Your grace does not leave me as I am but trains me. Teach me to deny ungodliness and worldly desires, and to live soberly, righteously and godly in this present age. Make me sound in faith, in love and in perseverance. Make me reverent, free from slander and from every kind of slavery to appetite. Help me to learn from those who are older in the faith and to encourage those who are younger. Let me be an example of good works, with teaching and speech that are sound and above reproach. Wherever I work, let my faithfulness adorn the teaching about You. I am looking for the blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Saviour, Jesus Christ, who gave Himself for me to redeem me from all lawlessness and to purify me as His own treasured possession. Make me zealous for good works. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Anna, a Reverent Older Woman","ref":"Luke 2:36-38","relatedVerses":"v. 3","summary":"When Mary and Joseph brought the baby Jesus to the temple, they met Anna, a prophetess who had been widowed young and had lived for decades in God's house. She “didn’t depart from the temple, worshiping with fastings and petitions night and day” (Luke 2:37). Her whole life was reverent, as Titus 2:3 describes. When she saw Jesus, she gave thanks to God and “spoke of him to all those who were looking for redemption in Jerusalem” (Luke 2:38). Anna is a picture of the older woman whose behaviour is fit for a temple, who looks for redemption, and who teaches others what is good."},{"title":"Priscilla Teaches Apollos","ref":"Acts 18:24-28","relatedVerses":"v. 3-5, 7","summary":"Apollos was an eloquent preacher in Ephesus who knew the Scriptures well but understood only the baptism of John. “But when Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him aside, and explained to him the way of God more accurately” (Acts 18:26). This married couple, who worked with Paul as tentmakers, discipled a gifted younger preacher privately and humbly, and Apollos went on to strengthen the churches in Achaia. Their home was a place of ministry and their marriage a partnership in the gospel. They show that teaching ‘that which is good’ often happens in homes, around tables, through patient mentoring."},{"title":"God's Treasured Possession at Sinai","ref":"Exodus 19:3-6","relatedVerses":"v. 14","summary":"Three months after the Exodus, Israel camped at the foot of Mount Sinai. God called Moses up the mountain and gave him a message for the people: He had carried them on eagles' wings and brought them to Himself. If they obeyed His voice and kept His covenant, they would be His own treasured possession among all peoples, a kingdom of priests and a holy nation. Paul takes up this language in Titus 2:14: Christ gave Himself to purify for Himself ‘a people for his own possession, zealous for good works’. Peter does the same, calling the church ‘a people for God’s own possession’. The redeemed people of God in Christ inherit the calling of Sinai."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Titus","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Saved by Mercy, Ready for Every Good Work","themeTitle":"“According to His Mercy, He Saved Us”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Remind them to be in subjection to rulers and to authorities, to be obedient, to be ready for every good work,","understanding":"ὑπομίμνῃσκε αὐτούς, “Remind them” — they knew it already; they needed reminding. ἀρχαῖς καὶ ἐξουσίαις ὑποτάσσεσθαι, “to be in subjection to rulers and to authorities.” Two members of the power family side by side: ἀρχή, rule, the authority of first place, and ἐξουσία, delegated right to act. Both are human government here. πειθαρχεῖν, “to be obedient” — to obey a ruler. πρὸς πᾶν ἔργον ἀγαθὸν ἑτοίμους εἶναι, “to be ready for every good work” — ἔργον from the work family, ἀγαθός from the good family. Believers are to be the best citizens in town, ready to help whenever good can be done. Crete had a reputation for rebellion; the church was to be different.","words":[{"strongs":"G5279","hebrew":"ὑπομιμνήσκω","translit":"hypomimnḗskō","english":"Remind"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"rulers"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authorities"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subjection"},{"strongs":"G3980","hebrew":"πειθαρχέω","translit":"peitharchéō","english":"obedient"},{"strongs":"G2092","hebrew":"ἕτοιμος","translit":"hétoimos","english":"ready"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"}]},{"v":2,"text":"to speak evil of no one, not to be contentious, to be gentle, showing all humility toward all men.","understanding":"μηδένα βλασφημεῖν, “to speak evil of no one.” A word-family point: ‘speak evil’ here is βλασφημέω, to slander, to damage someone's name — not κακός or πονηρός. ἀμάχους εἶναι, “not to be contentious” — not fighters; ἐπιεικεῖς, “to be gentle” — reasonable, yielding, ready to give way; πᾶσαν ἐνδεικνυμένους πραΰτητα πρὸς πάντας ἀνθρώπους, “showing all humility toward all men.” πραΰτης is meekness — strength under control. ‘All men’ includes rude neighbours and corrupt officials.","words":[{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"speak evil"},{"strongs":"G3367","hebrew":"μηδείς","translit":"mēdeís","english":"no one"},{"strongs":"G269","hebrew":"ἄμαχος","translit":"ámachos","english":"not to be contentious"},{"strongs":"G1933","hebrew":"ἐπιεικής","translit":"epieikḗs","english":"gentle"},{"strongs":"G1731","hebrew":"ἐνδείκνυμι","translit":"endeíknymi","english":"showing"},{"strongs":"G4240","hebrew":"πραΰτης","translit":"praÿtēs","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For we were also once foolish, disobedient, deceived, serving various lusts and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, and hating one another.","understanding":"Why be gentle with sinful outsiders? ἦμεν γάρ ποτε καὶ ἡμεῖς, “For we were also once” — Paul includes himself. ἀνόητοι, “foolish” — without understanding; ἀπειθεῖς, “disobedient”; πλανώμενοι, “deceived” — wandering off the path; δουλεύοντες ἐπιθυμίαις καὶ ἡδοναῖς ποικίλαις, “serving various lusts and pleasures” — δουλεύω from the servant family: we were slaves, and our masters were desires. ἐν κακίᾳ καὶ φθόνῳ διάγοντες, “living in malice and envy” — κακία from the evil family: malice, ill-will. στυγητοί, “hateful,” μισοῦντες ἀλλήλους, “and hating one another.” This is the ‘before’ picture of every believer.","words":[{"strongs":"G4218","hebrew":"ποτέ","translit":"poté","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G453","hebrew":"ἀνόητος","translit":"anóētos","english":"foolish"},{"strongs":"G545","hebrew":"ἀπειθής","translit":"apeithḗs","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G1398","hebrew":"δουλεύω","translit":"douleúō","english":"serving"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G2237","hebrew":"ἡδονή","translit":"hēdonḗ","english":"pleasures"},{"strongs":"G4164","hebrew":"ποικίλος","translit":"poikílos","english":"various"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"malice"},{"strongs":"G5355","hebrew":"φθόνος","translit":"phthónos","english":"envy"},{"strongs":"G4767","hebrew":"στυγνητός","translit":"stygnētós","english":"hateful"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hating"},{"strongs":"G240","hebrew":"ἀλλήλων","translit":"allḗlōn","english":"one another"}]},{"v":4,"text":"But when the kindness of God our Savior and his love toward mankind appeared,","understanding":"ὅτε δὲ ἡ χρηστότης καὶ ἡ φιλανθρωπία ἐπεφάνη τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν θεοῦ, “But when the kindness of God our Savior and his love toward mankind appeared.” Everything turns on ‘but’. χρηστότης is kindness, goodness that is useful to others. φιλανθρωπία, from the love family, is love for human beings (our ‘philanthropy’): the opposite of the hating and hateful people of verse 3. And ἐπιφαίνω from the appear family again (as in 2:11): God's kindness shone out like the dawn when Christ came.","words":[{"strongs":"G5544","hebrew":"χρηστότης","translit":"chrēstótēs","english":"kindness"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5363","hebrew":"φιλανθρωπία","translit":"philanthrōpía","english":"love toward mankind"},{"strongs":"G2014","hebrew":"ἐπιφαίνω","translit":"epiphaínō","english":"appeared"}]},{"v":5,"text":"not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit,","understanding":"οὐκ ἐξ ἔργων τῶν ἐν δικαιοσύνῃ ὧν ἐποιήσαμεν ἡμεῖς, “not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves” — ἔργον from the work family. The letter that speaks so much of good works insists they play no part in saving us. ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὸν αὐτοῦ ἔλεον ἔσωσεν ἡμᾶς, “but according to his mercy, he saved us.” How? διὰ λουτροῦ παλιγγενεσίας καὶ ἀνακαινώσεως πνεύματος ἁγίου, “through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit.” λουτρόν is a bath; παλιγγενεσία, a new genesis, being born again; ἀνακαίνωσις, from the new family, renewal in kind (καινός). Many connect the ‘washing’ with baptism, others with the inner cleansing the Spirit gives; either way, the one who saves is God in His mercy, and the agent of new birth is the Holy Spirit.","words":[{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"did"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G3067","hebrew":"λουτρόν","translit":"loutrón","english":"washing"},{"strongs":"G3824","hebrew":"παλιγγενεσία","translit":"palingenesía","english":"regeneration"},{"strongs":"G342","hebrew":"ἀνακαίνωσις","translit":"anakaínōsis","english":"renewing"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"}]},{"v":6,"text":"whom he poured out on us richly, through Jesus Christ our Savior;","understanding":"οὗ ἐξέχεεν ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς πλουσίως, “whom he poured out on us richly” — ἐκχέω, the very word of Pentecost, when Peter said the exalted Christ had ‘poured out’ the promised Spirit. πλουσίως, abundantly: God did not ration the Spirit. διὰ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν, “through Jesus Christ our Savior.” Notice the whole Trinity in verses 4–6: God our Savior (the Father) saved us by the Holy Spirit, poured out through Jesus Christ our Savior.","words":[{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G4146","hebrew":"πλουσίως","translit":"plousíōs","english":"richly"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":7,"text":"that being justified by his grace, we might be made heirs according to the hope of eternal life.","understanding":"ἵνα δικαιωθέντες τῇ ἐκείνου χάριτι, “that being justified by his grace” — δικαιόω, declared righteous: the courtroom verdict, received as a gift. κληρονόμοι γενώμεθα, “we might be made heirs” — those who share an inheritance by lot. κατ᾽ ἐλπίδα ζωῆς αἰωνίου, “according to the hope of eternal life” — ζωή from the life family. The letter began with ‘hope of eternal life’ (1:2), and the gospel summary ends in the same place.","words":[{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2818","hebrew":"κληρονόμος","translit":"klēronómos","english":"heirs"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"}]},{"v":8,"text":"This saying is faithful, and concerning these things I desire that you affirm confidently, so that those who have believed God may be careful to maintain good works. These things are good and profitable to men;","understanding":"πιστὸς ὁ λόγος, “This saying is faithful” — the last of five ‘faithful sayings’ in the pastoral letters, pointing back to verses 4–7. καὶ περὶ τούτων βούλομαί σε διαβεβαιοῦσθαι, “and concerning these things I desire that you affirm confidently” — insist on them. Why? ἵνα φροντίζωσιν καλῶν ἔργων προΐστασθαι οἱ πεπιστευκότες θεῷ, “so that those who have believed God may be careful to maintain good works.” Salvation is not by works, as verse 5 insisted; but it is always for works. καλός from the good family and ἔργον from the work family. ταῦτά ἐστιν τὰ καλὰ καὶ ὠφέλιμα τοῖς ἀνθρώποις, “These things are good and profitable to men.”","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"saying"},{"strongs":"G1011","hebrew":"βουλεύω","translit":"bouleúō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G1226","hebrew":"διαβεβαιόομαι","translit":"diabebaióomai","english":"affirm confidently"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"},{"strongs":"G5431","hebrew":"φροντίζω","translit":"phrontízō","english":"careful"},{"strongs":"G4291","hebrew":"προΐστημι","translit":"proḯstēmi","english":"maintain"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G5624","hebrew":"ὠφέλιμος","translit":"ōphélimos","english":"profitable"}]},{"v":9,"text":"but shun foolish questionings, genealogies, strife, and disputes about the law; for they are unprofitable and vain.","understanding":"μωρὰς δὲ ζητήσεις καὶ γενεαλογίας καὶ ἔρεις καὶ μάχας νομικὰς περιΐστασο, “but shun foolish questionings, genealogies, strife, and disputes about the law” — περιΐστημι, step around, give a wide berth. These were the false teachers' speciality (1:10, 14). εἰσὶν γὰρ ἀνωφελεῖς καὶ μάταιοι, “for they are unprofitable and vain.” Notice the deliberate contrast: good works are ὠφέλιμα, ‘profitable’ (v. 8); these debates are ἀνωφελεῖς, ‘unprofitable’. Some activity looks spiritual but helps no one.","words":[{"strongs":"G3474","hebrew":"μωρός","translit":"mōrós","english":"foolish"},{"strongs":"G2214","hebrew":"ζήτησις","translit":"zḗtēsis","english":"questionings"},{"strongs":"G1076","hebrew":"γενεαλογία","translit":"genealogía","english":"genealogies"},{"strongs":"G2054","hebrew":"ἔρις","translit":"éris","english":"strife"},{"strongs":"G3544","hebrew":"νομικός","translit":"nomikós","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G3163","hebrew":"μάχη","translit":"máchē","english":"disputes"},{"strongs":"G4026","hebrew":"περιΐστημι","translit":"periḯstēmi","english":"shun"},{"strongs":"G512","hebrew":"ἀνωφελής","translit":"anōphelḗs","english":"unprofitable"},{"strongs":"G3152","hebrew":"μάταιος","translit":"mátaios","english":"vain"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Avoid a factious man after a first and second warning,","understanding":"αἱρετικὸν ἄνθρωπον, “a factious man” — αἱρετικός (the root of ‘heretic’) here means someone who causes divisions by pushing his own opinions, forming a party. μετὰ μίαν καὶ δευτέραν νουθεσίαν, “after a first and second warning” — νουθεσία, putting sense into the mind: patient admonition, given twice. παραιτοῦ, “Avoid” — decline further dealings. Patience comes first; separation comes last.","words":[{"strongs":"G141","hebrew":"αἱρετικός","translit":"hairetikós","english":"factious"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G3559","hebrew":"νουθεσία","translit":"nouthesía","english":"warning"},{"strongs":"G3868","hebrew":"παραιτέομαι","translit":"paraitéomai","english":"Avoid"}]},{"v":11,"text":"knowing that such a one is perverted and sins, being self-condemned.","understanding":"εἰδὼς ὅτι ἐξέστραπται ὁ τοιοῦτος, “knowing that such a one is perverted” — ἐκστρέφω, twisted inside out. καὶ ἁμαρτάνει, “and sins” — ἁμαρτάνω, the verb of the sin family's chief word: to miss the mark, and to keep on missing it (present tense). ὢν αὐτοκατάκριτος, “being self-condemned” — his refusal of repeated warnings passes sentence on himself. The church does not condemn him; he has condemned himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G1612","hebrew":"ἐκστρέφω","translit":"ekstréphō","english":"perverted"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G843","hebrew":"αὐτοκατάκριτος","translit":"autokatákritos","english":"self-condemned"}]},{"v":12,"text":"When I send Artemas to you, or Tychicus, be diligent to come to me to Nicopolis, for I have determined to winter there.","understanding":"ὅταν πέμψω Ἀρτεμᾶν πρός σε ἢ Τυχικόν, “When I send Artemas to you, or Tychicus” — one of them would replace Titus on Crete. Tychicus later went to Ephesus (2 Timothy 4:12). σπούδασον ἐλθεῖν πρός με εἰς Νικόπολιν, “be diligent to come to me to Nicopolis” — probably Nicopolis in Epirus, on the western coast of Greece. ἐκεῖ γὰρ κέκρικα παραχειμάσαι, “for I have determined to winter there” — sailing stopped in winter. Shortly afterwards Paul writes that Titus had gone to Dalmatia, just north of there (2 Timothy 4:10).","words":[{"strongs":"G734","hebrew":"Ἀρτεμάς","translit":"Artemás","english":"Artemas"},{"strongs":"G5190","hebrew":"Τυχικός","translit":"Tychikós","english":"Tychicus"},{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"be diligent"},{"strongs":"G3533","hebrew":"Νικόπολις","translit":"Nikópolis","english":"Nicopolis"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"determined"},{"strongs":"G3914","hebrew":"παραχειμάζω","translit":"paracheimázō","english":"winter"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Send Zenas, the lawyer, and Apollos on their journey speedily, that nothing may be lacking for them.","understanding":"Ζηνᾶν τὸν νομικὸν καὶ Ἀπολλῶν σπουδαίως πρόπεμψον, “Send Zenas, the lawyer, and Apollos on their journey speedily.” νομικός, ‘lawyer’, is the same word as the ‘disputes about the law’ in verse 9: not every expert in law is a troublemaker. Apollos was the eloquent teacher from Alexandria (Acts 18:24). προπέμπω means to equip travellers for the road with food, money and companions. ἵνα μηδὲν αὐτοῖς λείπῃ, “that nothing may be lacking for them” — λείπω, the word of 1:5: Titus came to fix what was lacking in the churches; the churches must make sure nothing is lacking for the missionaries.","words":[{"strongs":"G2211","hebrew":"Ζηνᾶς","translit":"Zēnâs","english":"Zenas"},{"strongs":"G3544","hebrew":"νομικός","translit":"nomikós","english":"lawyer"},{"strongs":"G625","hebrew":"Ἀπολλῶς","translit":"Apollōs","english":"Apollos"},{"strongs":"G4709","hebrew":"σπουδαίως","translit":"spoudaíōs","english":"speedily"},{"strongs":"G4311","hebrew":"προπέμπω","translit":"propémpō","english":"Send"},{"strongs":"G3007","hebrew":"λείπω","translit":"leípō","english":"lacking"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Let our people also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they may not be unfruitful.","understanding":"μανθανέτωσαν δὲ καὶ οἱ ἡμέτεροι, “Let our people also learn” — learning implies practice and habit. καλῶν ἔργων προΐστασθαι, “to maintain good works” — the phrase of verse 8 again. εἰς τὰς ἀναγκαίας χρείας, “for necessary uses” — real needs, such as helping Zenas and Apollos on their way. ἵνα μὴ ὦσιν ἄκαρποι, “that they may not be unfruitful.” The Cretans' proverb called them ‘idle’ (ἀργός, 1:12); the church is to be fruitful. The last instruction of the letter is about good works.","words":[{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G2251","hebrew":"ἡμέτερος","translit":"hēméteros","english":"our"},{"strongs":"G4291","hebrew":"προΐστημι","translit":"proḯstēmi","english":"maintain"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G316","hebrew":"ἀναγκαῖος","translit":"anankaîos","english":"necessary"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"uses"},{"strongs":"G175","hebrew":"ἄκαρπος","translit":"ákarpos","english":"unfruitful"}]},{"v":15,"text":"All who are with me greet you. Greet those who love us in faith. Grace be with you all. Amen.","understanding":"ἀσπάζονταί σε οἱ μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ πάντες, “All who are with me greet you.” ἄσπασαι τοὺς φιλοῦντας ἡμᾶς ἐν πίστει, “Greet those who love us in faith” — φιλέω from the love family: the warm love of friends, bound together ‘in faith’. The letter that began with the φιλανθρωπία of God (v. 4) ends with the φιλία of believers. ἡ χάρις μετὰ πάντων ὑμῶν, “Grace be with you all” — ‘you all’ is plural: the letter was for every believer on Crete. Ἀμήν.","words":[{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"greet"},{"strongs":"G5368","hebrew":"φιλέω","translit":"philéō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Before and After","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture two photographs side by side. In the first, a group of people stand in a dark room: their faces are hard, their fists clenched, some turned away from each other, chains hanging from their wrists marked ‘desire’, ‘pleasure’, ‘envy’. In the second, the same people stand in bright sunlight. The chains lie broken on the ground. Their faces are softened; they are smiling, embracing, serving one another. Between the two photographs is a single word written in light: ‘But’.","meaning":"“For we were also once foolish, disobedient, deceived, serving various lusts and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, and hating one another” (v. 3). “But when the kindness of God our Savior and his love toward mankind appeared” (v. 4), “according to his mercy, he saved us” (v. 5). Every believer has a ‘before’ and an ‘after’, and between them is God's mercy. Remembering your own ‘before’ makes you gentle with others who are still in the dark."},{"title":"The Bath and the River","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture someone covered in the grime of years, stained deep into the skin, who has tried every soap and scrubbing brush without success. Then they are led to a pool of living water and plunged in. They rise clean — not just on the surface but through and through, as if they had been born again. And as they stand on the bank, a great river begins to pour down from above, not in drops but in torrents, flooding them with life.","meaning":"God “saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit, whom he poured out on us richly, through Jesus Christ our Savior” (vv. 5–6). Salvation is a bath and a new birth: God cleanses what we could never clean and gives a new beginning we could never create. And the Spirit is not given sparingly but ‘richly’. Ask the Spirit to make you freshly aware of the cleansing and the new life He has given you."},{"title":"Fruit Trees in a Barren Land","verseRef":"v. 8, 14","image":"Picture a dry, stony hillside where nothing useful grows. Then picture a farmer planting a grove of fruit trees there, watering them and tending them year after year. In time the trees blossom and bear fruit. Hungry travellers stop to eat; neighbours carry away baskets; the whole valley is fed. The trees did not plant themselves, but now that they are planted, they bear fruit for others.","meaning":"God saved us “not by works” (v. 5), but He saved us so that we would be “careful to maintain good works” (v. 8). The letter's final command is: “Let our people also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they may not be unfruitful” (v. 14). We are trees planted by grace, meant to feed others. Ask the Spirit where the ‘necessary uses’ around you are, and how you can bear fruit for them."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Good Citizens and Gentle Neighbours (v. 1-2)","body":"Believers must be reminded to submit to rulers and authorities, to be obedient and ready for every good work, to slander no one, to avoid fighting, and to be gentle and humble toward all people."},{"label":"Saved by God's Mercy (v. 3-7)","body":"We too were once foolish, disobedient, deceived and enslaved to desires, living in malice, envy and hatred; but when God our Saviour's kindness and love for humanity appeared, He saved us — not because of our works but by His mercy — through the washing of new birth and renewal by the Holy Spirit, poured out richly through Jesus Christ, so that, justified by grace, we might become heirs of eternal life."},{"label":"Devoted to Good Works, Avoiding Divisions (v. 8-11)","body":"This trustworthy saying must be stressed so that believers devote themselves to good works, which are good and profitable; foolish controversies, genealogies and quarrels about the law must be avoided as useless, and a divisive person warned twice and then avoided."},{"label":"Final Instructions and Greetings (v. 12-15)","body":"Titus is to come to Paul at Nicopolis when Artemas or Tychicus arrives, to help Zenas and Apollos on their journey, and to teach the believers to devote themselves to good works that meet real needs; Paul sends greetings and grace to all."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Gentle Toward All, Because We Were Once Like Them","paragraphs":["Chapter 3 turns from life in the church to life in the world. Titus is to “Remind them to be in subjection to rulers and to authorities, to be obedient, to be ready for every good work, to speak evil of no one, not to be contentious, to be gentle, showing all humility toward all men” (vv. 1–2). Believers on an island known for lying and rebellion are to be model citizens: respectful, helpful, peaceable, gentle. Paul taught the same in Romans: “Let every soul be in subjection to the higher authorities, for there is no authority except from God” (Romans 13:1).","Why should Christians be gentle with a corrupt and hostile world? Because “we were also once foolish, disobedient, deceived, serving various lusts and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, and hating one another” (v. 3). Paul does not say ‘they are’ but ‘we were’. He describes himself, the former blasphemer and persecutor, as much as anyone. To the Ephesians he wrote, “among whom we also all once lived in the lusts of our flesh” (Ephesians 2:3). Believers have no right to look down on anyone.","Remembering where we came from produces humility, and humility produces gentleness. The word for ‘gentle’ (ἐπιεικής) describes someone who does not insist on their rights; ‘humility’ (πραΰτης) is strength under control. This is not weakness, but the character of Christ. Paul urges the same elsewhere: “Let your gentleness be known to all men. The Lord is at hand” (Philippians 4:5). The gospel changes how we treat those who are still what we once were."]},{"heading":"But When the Kindness of God Appeared","paragraphs":["Then comes one of the greatest sentences in Scripture: “But when the kindness of God our Savior and his love toward mankind appeared, not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit” (vv. 4–5). The ‘but’ is God's intervention. Into a world of hatred came God's φιλανθρωπία, His love for human beings, shining out in Christ. Paul wrote similarly to the Ephesians: “But God, being rich in mercy, for his great love with which he loved us” (Ephesians 2:4).","Salvation is entirely God's work. It is ‘not by works’ — not our religious efforts, not our moral record. It is ‘according to his mercy’. And it is a total transformation: a washing that cleanses and a new birth that begins a new life, worked by the Holy Spirit. Jesus told Nicodemus, “unless one is born of water and spirit, he can’t enter into God’s Kingdom” (John 3:5). Paul reminded the Corinthians, “but you were washed. But you were sanctified. But you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus, and in the Spirit of our God” (1 Corinthians 6:11).","God “poured out on us richly” this Spirit “through Jesus Christ our Savior” (v. 6), “that being justified by his grace, we might be made heirs according to the hope of eternal life” (v. 7). The Father, the Son and the Spirit work together in our salvation. We are justified — declared righteous by grace — and made heirs. Paul says in Romans, “and if children, then heirs; heirs of God, and joint heirs with Christ” (Romans 8:17). The beggar becomes a son with an inheritance."]},{"heading":"Careful to Maintain Good Works","paragraphs":["Immediately after this glorious statement of grace, Paul says: “This saying is faithful, and concerning these things I desire that you affirm confidently, so that those who have believed God may be careful to maintain good works” (v. 8). Grace is not the enemy of good works; it is their source. We are saved not by works but for works. Paul says it plainly to the Ephesians: “For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works” (Ephesians 2:10).","In contrast, Titus must avoid what is useless: “but shun foolish questionings, genealogies, strife, and disputes about the law; for they are unprofitable and vain” (v. 9). Good works are profitable; these debates profit no one. And a divisive person, after two warnings, must be avoided, because “such a one is perverted and sins, being self-condemned” (v. 11). The church must protect its unity and its time for what really matters.","The letter ends with travel plans and greetings, but even here good works appear. Titus is to help Zenas and Apollos on their journey “that nothing may be lacking for them” (v. 13). And the last instruction is, “Let our people also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they may not be unfruitful” (v. 14). Jesus said, “In this is my Father glorified, that you bear much fruit” (John 15:8). The church on Crete, among people called idle, was to be known for fruitful, practical love."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Mercy, New Birth and the Spirit Poured Out","paragraphs":["Titus 3:4–7 is a summary of the gospel that echoes across the New Testament. Paul wrote to the Ephesians, “for by grace you have been saved through faith, and that not of yourselves; it is the gift of God” (Ephesians 2:8), “not of works, that no one would boast” (Ephesians 2:9). Believers are “justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus” (Romans 3:24). And “if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation” (2 Corinthians 5:17).","The Spirit poured out richly fulfils the promise quoted by Peter at Pentecost: “It will be in the last days, says God, that I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh” (Acts 2:17). Peter explained that the risen Christ, “having received from the Father the promise of the Holy Spirit, he has poured out this, which you now see and hear” (Acts 2:33). Paul says the same of God's love: “God’s love has been poured out into our hearts through the Holy Spirit who was given to us” (Romans 5:5).","The instruction to be subject to authorities and gentle toward all fits the wider apostolic teaching. Peter writes, “Therefore subject yourselves to every ordinance of man for the Lord’s sake” (1 Peter 2:13). Paul urges, “If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men” (Romans 12:18). And he warns the Romans to “look out for those who are causing the divisions and occasions of stumbling, contrary to the doctrine which you learned, and turn away from them” (Romans 16:17), much as Titus is told to deal with the factious man."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Love toward mankind / philanthropy","hebrew":"φιλανθρωπία","translit":"philanthrōpía","strongs":"G5363","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “fondness of mankind, i.e. benevolence (\"philanthropy\").” In Greek culture it was praised in kings who were generous to their subjects. Luke uses it of the islanders of Malta who welcomed shipwrecked Paul. Here it is God's own love for humanity, appearing in Christ. Set against the hateful, hating people of verse 3, it shows that God loved people who did not love Him or one another."},{"english":"Regeneration / new birth","hebrew":"παλιγγενεσία","translit":"palingenesía","strongs":"G3824","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “(spiritual) rebirth (the state or the act), i.e. (figuratively) spiritual renovation; specially, Messianic restoration.” It combines πάλιν, again, and γένεσις, birth or beginning. Jesus used it of the renewal of all things at His return (Matthew 19:28). Paul uses it of the new birth of the believer. What God will one day do for the whole creation, He has already begun in everyone who is in Christ."},{"english":"Renewing / renovation","hebrew":"ἀνακαίνωσις","translit":"anakaínōsis","strongs":"G342","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “renovation.” It is built from ἀνά, again, and καινός, new in quality, the first member of the ‘new’ word family. Paul uses it in Romans 12:2 for the renewing of the mind. Regeneration is the new beginning; renewal is the ongoing work of the Spirit making us new in kind. Both come from God's mercy, not our effort."},{"english":"Pour out","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","strongs":"G1632","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “to pour forth; figuratively, to bestow.” It is used of blood poured out, of wine spilled and of God's Spirit poured out at Pentecost. Paul adds πλουσίως, ‘richly’. God does not ration His Spirit or give Him reluctantly. The same generous God who saved us by mercy pours His Spirit on us abundantly, through Jesus Christ."},{"english":"Unfruitful / barren","hebrew":"ἄκαρπος","translit":"ákarpos","strongs":"G175","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “barren (literally or figuratively).” It is used of seed choked by thorns in the parable of the sower, and of the ‘unfruitful deeds of darkness’ in Ephesians. Paul's final instruction to Titus is that believers learn good works so they are not barren. A life saved by grace is meant to bear fruit that meets real needs: food for the hungry, help for travellers, kindness for neighbours."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"As this letter closes, the Holy Spirit speaks to you about remembering your ‘before’, rejoicing in God's mercy, and bearing fruit.","items":[{"title":"Remember What You Were","body":"The Spirit reminds you that you too were once foolish, deceived and enslaved. This is not to shame you, but to make you gentle with others who are still where you were. Ask Him to replace any harshness or superiority toward unbelievers with the humility and kindness of someone who has been rescued."},{"title":"Rest in His Mercy","body":"You were saved not by works of righteousness you did, but by His mercy. The Spirit wants to free you from trying to earn God's love. Let Him wash away any lingering sense that you must prove yourself to God. You have been washed, reborn, renewed, justified and made an heir. Receive it with gratitude."},{"title":"Bear Fruit for Real Needs","body":"The Spirit has been poured out richly on you so that you may bear fruit. He wants your life to be practical and useful to others. Ask Him to show you the ‘necessary uses’ around you this week — people with real needs He wants you to meet."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Citizenship Check","body":"How do you speak about leaders and authorities, and about people you disagree with? Do your words ‘speak evil of no one’ (v. 2)? Identify one habit of speech to change."},{"title":"The Before-and-After Check","body":"Write down two or three things that described your life before Christ (v. 3), and what God has changed by His mercy (vv. 4–7). Thank Him for each change."},{"title":"The Profitable Check","body":"List how you spent your spiritual energy this past week. Mark what was ‘profitable’ (good works, v. 8) and what was ‘unprofitable’ (empty arguments, v. 9). Choose to shift one hour from the second to the first."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Memorise the Gospel of Titus 3:4–7","body":"Commit Titus 3:4–7 to memory over the next two weeks. Say it every morning as a reminder that you are saved by mercy, washed, renewed, justified and made an heir. When you feel condemned or proud, recite it again. Share it with someone who is struggling to believe God could accept them."},{"title":"Meet a Necessary Need","body":"This month, look for a real, practical need in your church or community — a missionary or traveller who needs support, a family facing hardship, a neighbour who needs help. Meet it generously, as Titus was to equip Zenas and Apollos ‘that nothing may be lacking for them’ (v. 13). Invite others to join you, so that ‘our people also learn to maintain good works’."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I remember that I too was once foolish, disobedient and deceived, enslaved to my desires, living in malice and envy, hateful and hating others. But when Your kindness and Your love for humanity appeared in Jesus Christ, You saved me — not because of works of righteousness I had done, but because of Your mercy. Thank You for the washing of new birth and the renewing of the Holy Spirit, whom You poured out on me richly through Jesus Christ my Saviour. Thank You that I am justified by Your grace and made an heir of eternal life. Let this truth make me gentle and humble toward everyone, respectful to those in authority, slow to speak evil of anyone, and ready for every good work. Keep me from foolish arguments and divisions. Teach me to devote myself to good works that meet real needs, so that my life is not unfruitful. Grace be with me and with all Your people. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Saul the Persecutor Becomes Paul the Apostle","ref":"Acts 9:1-19","relatedVerses":"v. 3-7","summary":"Saul was ‘breathing threats and slaughter’ against the disciples, travelling to Damascus to arrest believers. He was the very picture of Titus 3:3: deceived, disobedient, filled with hatred. Then “suddenly a light from the sky shone around him” (Acts 9:3), and he heard Jesus' voice: “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?” (Acts 9:4). Blinded, he was led into the city. Three days later Ananias laid hands on him, and “something like scales fell from his eyes, and he received his sight. He arose and was baptized” (Acts 9:18). Paul later wrote that he had been “a blasphemer, a persecutor, and insolent. However, I obtained mercy” (1 Timothy 1:13). When he wrote ‘we were also once…’ in Titus 3:3, he was describing himself."},{"title":"Apollos Sent on His Way","ref":"Acts 18:24-28","relatedVerses":"v. 13","summary":"Apollos was “a certain Jew named Apollos, an Alexandrian by race, an eloquent man” (Acts 18:24) who knew the Scriptures well. After Priscilla and Aquila taught him more accurately, he wanted to go to Achaia, and the believers in Ephesus encouraged him and wrote to the disciples there to welcome him. In Corinth he greatly helped those who had believed. Paul later wrote, “I planted. Apollos watered. But God gave the increase” (1 Corinthians 3:6). In Titus 3:13, Apollos is travelling again, and the church on Crete is to supply him generously so that nothing is lacking. Supporting gospel workers on their journeys is one of the ‘good works for necessary uses’."},{"title":"The Kindness of the Islanders of Malta","ref":"Acts 28:1-10","relatedVerses":"v. 4, 14","summary":"After Paul's ship was wrecked on the voyage to Rome — having sailed past Crete — the survivors struggled ashore on Malta, cold and soaked. Luke records that “The natives showed us uncommon kindness” (Acts 28:2) — the word is φιλανθρωπία — building a fire and welcoming them all. Paul then healed the father of Publius, the chief man of the island, and many others. When they left, “they put on board the things that we needed” (Acts 28:10). The story shows kindness meeting real needs, and gospel power flowing through a prisoner. It is a small picture of God's own φιλανθρωπία, which ‘appeared’ in Christ, and of the practical good works Titus 3 calls for."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"Titus","titleEmojis":"🏝️✨","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Grace That Saves, Grace That Trains, Grace That Bears Fruit","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of Titus in one overarching thought, it is this: because the God who cannot lie has made His kindness and love for humanity appear in Jesus Christ — saving us not by our works but by His mercy, through the washing of new birth and the renewing of the Holy Spirit — His people must be led by godly elders, taught healthy doctrine, trained by grace to live sober-minded, righteous and godly lives, and devoted to good works that adorn the gospel and meet real needs, while they wait for the appearing of the glory of our great God and Saviour, Jesus Christ.","paragraphs":["Titus is a short letter with a big agenda. Paul had left Titus on Crete, where young churches were scattered across an island known for lies, brutality and laziness. False teachers, especially from the circumcision party, were overturning households for money with myths, genealogies and human rules. Titus's task was to finish setting the churches in order: appoint godly elders, silence error, and teach the believers how to live. The letter gives him his instructions in three chapters, one for the church's leaders, one for its households, and one for its life in the world.","The first key is that truth leads to godliness. Paul writes for “the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (1:1). The false teachers “profess that they know God, but by their deeds they deny him” (1:16). In contrast, elders must hold to “the faithful word which is according to the teaching” (1:9), and every group in the church must live lives that “fit sound doctrine” (2:1). ‘Sound’ means healthy: true teaching produces healthy lives, and the beauty of those lives makes the teaching attractive.","The second key is grace. Twice Paul gives a summary of the gospel. “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men, instructing us” (2:11–12) to deny ungodliness and live godly lives while we wait for Christ's appearing. And when God's kindness and love for humanity appeared, “not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit” (3:5). Grace saves, and grace trains.","The third key is good works. No New Testament book of its size mentions them so often. Elders must be lovers of good; Titus must be “an example of good works” (2:7); Christ died to purify “a people for his own possession, zealous for good works” (2:14); believers must be “ready for every good work” (3:1) and “careful to maintain good works” (3:8). The final instruction is “Let our people also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they may not be unfruitful” (3:14). Saved by mercy, believers become a fruitful people."]},"map":{"intro":"Titus is the seventeenth book of the New Testament and the third of Paul's ‘pastoral letters’. It was probably written in the mid 60s AD, after Paul's release from his first Roman imprisonment and before his final arrest, while he planned to winter at Nicopolis (3:12). In our Bibles it follows 2 Timothy, though it was most likely written before it.","sections":[{"title":"A Smaller 1 Timothy with a Sharper Focus","body":"Titus shares much with 1 Timothy: qualifications for elders (1:6–9; compare 1 Timothy 3), warnings about false teachers with myths and genealogies, and instructions for different groups in the church. But Titus is more compact and more focused on outsiders. Again and again Paul gives the reason for a command: that God's word not be blasphemed (2:5), that opponents have nothing evil to say (2:8), that the teaching be adorned (2:10). The church's conduct is its witness."},{"title":"Two Great Summaries of the Gospel","body":"Titus contains two of the richest compressed statements of the gospel in the New Testament: 2:11–14 and 3:4–7. The first emphasises grace that trains and the hope of Christ's return; the second emphasises mercy that saves through the Spirit's new birth. Together they cover past, present and future: God saved us, grace trains us now, and we wait for the appearing of our great God and Saviour. Both summaries lead straight into good works."},{"title":"Words That Need Care","body":"Several passages in Titus need careful reading. ‘Having children who believe’ (1:6) may mean ‘faithful’ children. The Cretan proverb (1:12) is applied to the false teachers' behaviour, not to every Cretan. The instructions to younger wives (2:4–5) and to slaves (2:9–10) are given with a missionary purpose, alongside Paul's calls to husbands and masters elsewhere. 2:13 is best read as calling Jesus ‘our great God and Savior’. And the ‘washing of regeneration’ (3:5) is related to baptism by some and to the Spirit's inner cleansing by others. This study has tried to give the strongest reading and to say plainly where uncertainty remains."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: Titus ended with grace for ‘you all’ and a call to good works. The next two books are Philemon, Paul's short personal appeal for a runaway slave who has become a brother; and Hebrews, the great letter showing that Jesus is better than everything that came before Him.","books":[{"book":"Philemon","connection":"Philemon is the shortest of Paul's letters and the last of the letters to individuals. It begins, “Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy our brother, to Philemon, our beloved fellow worker” (Philemon 1:1). Titus 2 taught slaves to adorn the gospel and Titus 3 described the kindness of God that saves the undeserving. Philemon shows both in one real household. Onesimus, a slave who had wronged Philemon and run away, met Paul in prison and became a believer. Paul sends him back and asks Philemon to receive him ‘no longer as a slave, but more than a slave, a beloved brother’. The gospel of mercy reshapes a broken relationship. (This study will quote Philemon in full once its text has been re-fetched.)"},{"book":"Hebrews","connection":"Hebrews leaves Paul's personal letters behind and opens with majestic words: “God, having in the past spoken to the fathers through the prophets at many times and in various ways” (Hebrews 1:1), “has at the end of these days spoken to us by his Son” (Hebrews 1:2). Titus called Jesus ‘our great God and Savior’; Hebrews shows His greatness in detail: “His Son is the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance” (Hebrews 1:3), better than angels, Moses, the priesthood and the old sacrifices. Titus spoke of a people purified for God; Hebrews explains how Christ “had by himself purified us of our sins” (Hebrews 1:3). And Titus's zeal for good works continues: “Let us consider how to provoke one another to love and good works” (Hebrews 10:24). Watch for the word ‘better’, and for the call to hold fast."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"Truth That Leads to Godliness","anchorRef":"Titus 1:1","body":"Paul's ministry serves “the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (1:1). True knowledge of God is never merely in the head; it produces a godly life. False teachers profess to know God but deny Him by their deeds (1:16). The test of what you believe is how you live. Sound doctrine and sound living belong together."},{"title":"The God Who Cannot Lie","anchorRef":"Titus 1:2-3","body":"Eternal life was promised by “God, who can’t lie” (1:2) before time began, and revealed “in his own time” (1:3). The word for time in verse 2 is χρόνος, the long ages; in verse 3 it is καιρός, the right moment. Among people known as ‘always liars’, the gospel stands on the absolute truthfulness of God. Every promise He has made will be kept at the right time."},{"title":"Godly Leaders, Healthy Churches","anchorRef":"Titus 1:5-9","body":"Titus was to “set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city” (1:5). Elders are God's stewards: blameless in family and character, not arrogant, angry, drunken, violent or greedy, but hospitable, lovers of good, sound-minded, just, holy and self-controlled, holding firmly to the faithful word. Healthy churches need leaders whose lives match the truth they teach."},{"title":"Grace That Trains Us to Live Soberly","anchorRef":"Titus 2:11-14","body":"“For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men” (2:11), training us to deny ungodliness and worldly lusts and to live “soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (2:12). The σώφρων words — sober-minded, self-controlled, of sound mind — run through the whole chapter for every age group. Grace is not only a pardon but a tutor, and it points us to the blessed hope of Christ's appearing."},{"title":"Saved by Mercy, Devoted to Good Works","anchorRef":"Titus 3:4-8","body":"“Not by works of righteousness which we did ourselves, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing by the Holy Spirit” (3:5). Justified by grace, we are heirs of eternal life. And precisely because we are saved by mercy, believers must be “careful to maintain good works” (3:8). Good works are not the root of salvation but its fruit, meeting real needs and adorning the gospel."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of Titus practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":1,"title":"Let Your Life Fit Your Doctrine","lesson":"The Lesson: “But say the things which fit sound doctrine” (2:1). Healthy teaching should produce a healthy life, and the false teachers' tragedy was that their deeds denied the God they professed.","practice":"The Practice: Choose three things you firmly believe about God — for example, His truthfulness, His grace, His coming. For each, write one way your behaviour this week should reflect it. Review at the end of each day. After a month, ask a trusted friend whether they see your life fitting your beliefs, and listen humbly to their answer."},{"step":2,"title":"Practise Sober-Mindedness Across Generations","lesson":"The Lesson: Older men and women, younger women and men, and Titus himself are all called to the σώφρων life (2:2–6), and the older are to train the younger. Grace teaches us to live “soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (2:12).","practice":"The Practice: Identify one area of your life that most needs self-control (speech, appetite, spending, screen time, temper). Set one clear, realistic boundary for thirty days. At the same time, seek out someone older in the faith to learn from and someone younger to encourage. Meet with each at least twice this month and talk honestly about what grace is training you to deny and to embrace."},{"step":3,"title":"Become Zealous for Good Works","lesson":"The Lesson: Christ died to purify “a people for his own possession, zealous for good works” (2:14), and the letter's last word is, “Let our people also learn to maintain good works for necessary uses, that they may not be unfruitful” (3:14).","practice":"The Practice: Each week for the next two months, plan and do one practical good work that meets a real need — a meal, a repair, a visit, a gift, support for a missionary or traveller, help for someone in your workplace. Keep a simple record. Invite family or friends to join you in some of them, so that ‘our people also learn’. At the end, thank God that His mercy has made your life fruitful."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father, I close Your letter to Titus, and I declare: I believe the knowledge of the truth which leads to godliness. You are the God who cannot lie. You promised eternal life before time began, and at the right time You revealed Your word in the gospel. I honour the leaders You give Your church, and I pray that they — and I — will be blameless, hospitable, lovers of good, sober-minded, just, holy and self-controlled, holding fast to Your faithful word. I refuse myths, empty talk and human rules that turn from the truth. My deeds will not deny the God my lips confess. Whatever my age or station, I will live in a way that fits sound doctrine, and my faithfulness will adorn the teaching about God my Saviour. The grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all people, and it is training me to deny ungodliness and worldly desires and to live soberly, righteously and godly in this present age. I am waiting for the blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Saviour, Jesus Christ, who gave Himself for me to redeem me from all lawlessness and to purify me as His own treasured possession, zealous for good works. I will be gentle and humble toward all, because I too was once foolish and enslaved. But when Your kindness and love for humanity appeared, You saved me — not by my works, but by Your mercy — through the washing of new birth and the renewing of the Holy Spirit, poured out on me richly through Jesus Christ. I am justified by grace and an heir of eternal life. I will devote myself to good works that meet real needs, so that my life is not unfruitful. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
